Are you a MSc graduate with background in data science, computer science, biostatistics, bioinformatics or a related field? Do you have a solid foundation in machine learning? Are you passionate about biology and interested in building new machine learning and AI to accelerate biological discoveries? Then this position is for you!
 Join Us! 
We are looking for a motivated PhD candidate to develop novel methodology for the analysis of single-cell (multi)-omics data by incorporating existing biological knowledge into machine learning models. You will join a collaborative and internationally-oriented team working at the interface of computer science, mathematics, and biology in the Biosystems Data Analysis group at the Swammerdam Institute for Life Sciences at the University of Amsterdam.
We welcome applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds, experiences and perspectives. If you recognise yourself in the role but do not meet every listed preference, we encourage you to apply.
 This is what you will do 
Interpretable machine learning is a growing research area, with important applications in the biological sciences, such as understanding how different genes regulate each other within biological pathways. However, the current common practice is first to build complicated, “black-box” models and try to understand what they learned afterwards. This has the disadvantage that a) interpretation is still not always possible and b) it is inefficient as the model must re-discover patterns that are already well-known from scratch every time it is trained.
Recent technological advances have allowed us to study DNA and RNA not only in a “bulk” tissue, but also at a single-cell resolution, giving us unprecedented insights into how organisms form, how diseases develop, and how cells communicate with each other inside a tissue.
In this project, you will build interpretable-by-design machine learning models for biological data that offer biological insights in a direct way. Such models will be applicable to a variety of single-cell datasets and experiments in different fields of biology.
 Tasks and responsibilities: 
 complete a PhD thesis within the official appointment duration (four years);
 develop novel algorithms for analyzing single-cell (multi)-omics data and design and perform in silico experiments to test them;
 be an active and responsible member of the research group and collaborate closely with fellow group members;
 discuss work with group members and at departmental meetings, and incorporate feedback;
 take a leading role in writing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals and conferences;
 participate in the PhD training programme of the University of Amsterdam;
 assist with teaching and supervision of Bachelor’s and Master’s students.
You will have the opportunity to:
 work at the interface of machine learning and biology;
 collaborate closely with biologists from a variety of disciplines including immunology and cancer biology;
 present results at national and international scientific conferences;
 expand academic, professional and personal skills;
 use high-end compute infrastructure via the national ICT cooperative (SURF)
 What we ask of you 
You are passionate about research and want to develop into an independent scientist. You have a background in machine learning and artificial intelligence and like to build novel methods for the analysis of biological data. You are methodical, curious and able to take initiative, while also valuing close collaboration in an interdisciplinary and international research environment.
 Your experience and profile 
You
 hold a MSc in Bioinformatics, Artificial Intelligence, Data Science, Biostatistics or a closely related discipline;
 have demonstrable experience with training machine and deep learning models, preferably using Python;
 have a basic understanding of biology and/or are interested in using computational methods to understand biology;
 are fluent in English, written and spoken.
 Experience with single-cell transcriptomics data analysis and working with high-performance computing systems are considered a plus. 
 This is what we offer you 
A temporary contract for 38 hours per week for the duration of 4 years (the initial contract will be for a period of 18 months and after satisfactory evaluation it will be extended for a total duration of 4 years). This should lead to a dissertation (PhD thesis). We will draft an educational plan that includes attendance of courses and (international) meetings. We also expect you to assist in teaching undergraduates and master students.
Your salary will range between €3,204 in the first year to €4,051 gross per month in the last year of employment, based on a fulltime employment (38 hours per week). This sum does not include the 8% holiday pay and the 8.3% end-of-year bonus. A favorable tax agreement, the 30% ruling, may apply to non-Dutch applicants. The Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universitie

academictransfer:364171.description:
In this postdoctoral project, you will develop innovative multimodal AI frameworks to integrate diverse cardiovascular data sources. Your research will focus on combining imaging data (e.g., cardiac MRI, echocardiography), signals (e.g. electrocardiography, intracardiac signals), clinical measurements, molecular and genomic profiles to improve diagnostic accuracy and patient stratification in cardiovascular diseases. You will work on synthetic data generation techniques to improve multi-centre training while maintaining privacy, and develop methods to optimize cross-modal representations for enhanced predictive modeling.
As a postdoctoral researcher, you will:
 Design and implement novel multimodal AI architectures for cardiovascular applications;
 Develop synthetic data generation methods to augment sparse modalities;
 Create frameworks for joint optimization of cross-modal representations;
 Validate models using clinical datasets in collaboration with cardiologists;
 Mentor PhD and MSc students and present findings at international conferences;
 Intention to apply for grants to expand our group, and potentially start your own research group.

REQUIREMENTS:
You have a PhD in Computer Science, AI/ML, Biomedical Engineering, or related field;
 You demonstrate strong programming skills in Python and have experience with deep learning frameworks such as PyTorch; and having experience with high performance computing infrastructures is a plus;
 You have experience in medical image analysis (multimodal learning experience is a plus);
 You have a publication track record in top AI/ML or medical informatics venues such as NeurIPS, ICLR, MICCAI, or SPIE Medical Imaging; and/or in translational venues such as Nature portfolio journals (Nature Medicine, Nature Cardiovascular Medicine, npj Digital Medicine)
 Experience with clinical healthcare data systems is a plus.

academictransfer:364180.description:
Make more donor livers transplantable through cutting-edge transplantation research
Improving the quality and availability of donor organs is one of the greatest challenges in modern transplantation. At the University of Groningen and UMCG, you will contribute to the development of next-generation technologies aimed at assessing, protecting, and repairing donor livers before transplantation. This PhD project is embedded within the multidisciplinary CRISPR-CRYSTAL consortium, bringing together experts in transplantation, nanotechnology, quantum sensing, mitochondrial medicine, and epigenetic editing. The project combines fundamental science with a clear path toward clinical translation, offering the opportunity to make a direct impact on patient care.
 About your role 
During machine perfusion, donor livers can be maintained outside the body, creating a unique opportunity to assess organ quality and deliver targeted therapies before transplantation. In this PhD project, you will develop and evaluate innovative strategies to detect, prevent, and repair oxidative stress–associated injury in high-risk donor livers.
The project integrates three complementary approaches:
- real-time quantum nanodiamond sensing of oxidative stress;
- mitochondrial protection using the clinical-stage compound SUL-238;
- CRISPR-based epigenetic editing delivered via lipid nanoparticles;
Using a combination of cell models and human donor livers, you will investigate how these technologies can improve organ quality and increase the number of transplantable donor livers. 
 Your main responsibilities 
- Performing (long-term) ex vivo liver perfusion experiments using hypothermic and normothermic machine perfusion platforms.
- Evaluating mitochondrial function, oxidative stress, and liver viability during perfusion.
- Testing nanoparticle-based delivery systems and CRISPR-based epigenetic therapies.
- Analyzing and interpreting experimental data and presenting findings at scientific meetings.
- Collaborating closely with academic and industrial consortium partners.
- Contributing to scientific publications and ensuring that the research results in a PhD thesis.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for an enthusiastic and highly motivated candidate with a strong interest in organ transplantation and ex situ machine perfusion, regenerative medicine, and translational biomedical research. You enjoy experimental work, can work independently, and are eager to collaborate across disciplines.
 You have: 
- a Master’s degree in Biomedical Sciences/Engineering, Biology, Medicine, Pharmacy, or a related field;
- an interest in organ transplantation and machine perfusion;
- strong analytical and organizational skills;
- excellent communication skills in English;
- affinity with translational and interdisciplinary research;
- basic laboratory skills, including pipetting, cell culture, and molecular biology techniques, or a willingness to acquire these skills;
- a proactive, independent, and collaborative working style.
Experience with organ perfusion, nanoparticle delivery, CRISPR technologies, is considered an advantage but is not required.

academictransfer:364192.description:
The Wadden Sea and the Ems-Dollard and Western Scheldt estuaries have nationally important navigation channels and globally important ecosystems on tidal flats and coastal wetlands, that also protect the coast against storms. However, dredging costs are escalating, biodiversity is declining, and the sea level is rising. These three threats are magnified by a surplus or deficit of sand and mud, which requires sediment management. The knowledge and tools needed for this pose profound scientific challenges.
 Your job 
The aim of the WadSED project is to develop the urgently needed system understanding, data analysis tools, and forecasting models for science and expert users, and co-create common ground among stakeholders for future-proof coastal management.
One the project outcomes is to explore and co-develop narratives and conceptual models that show development of the basins and estuaries under SLR and human interference with specific attention for the sediment budget and habitat development.
As a postdoctoral researcher you will organize and facilitate the participatory process to co-develop these shared narratives and conceptual models for sustainable sediment management. This will be done for two case studies, namely Western Scheldt and Wadden Sea. To facilitate knowledge exchange and the learning process, interactive workshops will be organized on location (Western Scheldt and Wadden Sea) to enhance science-society interactions.
 Responsibilities 
The primary responsibilities of the postdoctoral researcher are to engage with the project team members and the participating experts and stakeholders for both case studies to coordinate, facilitate and monitor the co-creation process to develop shared narratives and conceptual model for sustainable sediment management.
This will be achieved through:
 Designing and organizing a deliberative and participatory approach together with the WadSED team members (specifically UU and HVHL).
 Facilitating and monitoring the co-creation process with experts and stakeholders in Western Scheldt and Wadden Sea.
 Applying a research for policy cycle strategy to co-create new conceptual models with academic scholars, experts, stakeholders and policy makers.
 Facilitating knowledge exchange between researchers, experts, stakeholders and policymakers through stakeholder workshops.
 Disseminating best practices via communication strategies that target experts, policy makers, and the public.
The selected candidate is expected to contribute to the above-mentioned tasks in collaboration with the rest of the WadSED team members.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are searching for a candidate with the following qualifications:
 Fluency in Dutch and English;
 A PhD degree in social and environmental policy sciences or related disciplines;
 Expertise and experience working in facilitating deliberative and participatory processes in social- ecological systems;
 Affinity with the water management sector;
 Demonstrable skills in qualitative research and scientific writing;
 Strong communication skills and preferably expertise with learning design (for example visual media production project);
 Knowledge of mediated participation in a context of natural resource management transitions is an advantage;
 Cross-cultural sensitivity and a team player attitude, willing to be part of a large national;
 A driving license might come in handy.

academictransfer:364193.description:
Zorg jij graag voor een foutloze salarisverwerking? En wil je bijdragen aan een organisatie die draait om onderwijs en onderzoek?
Als medewerker salarisadministratie zorg je samen met je collega’s voor een correcte en tijdige verwerking van de personeels- en salarisgegevens van meer dan 9.000 medewerkers. Je werkt nauwkeurig en klantgericht, binnen een professionele organisatie waar collegialiteit en ontwikkeling centraal staan.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Je verwerkt salarismutaties en controleert gegevens in SAP op juistheid en volledigheid. Je beantwoordt vragen van collega’s over de salarisverwerking en past wet- en regelgeving correct toe. Daarnaast draag je bij aan het verbeteren en automatiseren van onze HR- en salarisprocessen.
Jouw werkzaamheden:
 verwerken van salarismutaties in SAP;
 controleren van gegevens op juistheid en volledigheid;
 uitvoeren van proefberekeningen;
 beantwoorden van vragen over salarisverwerking van collega’s en leidinggevenden;
 toepassen van wet- en regelgeving, cao’s en interne afspraken;
 opstellen van rapportages;
 bijdragen aan de digitalisering en automatisering van HR- en salarisprocessen;
 signaleren van knelpunten in processen en aandragen van verbetervoorstellen.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je communiceert helder, werkt nauwkeurig en houdt het hoofd koel in drukke periodes. Je werkt graag samen en denkt mee over hoe processen slimmer en efficiënter kunnen.
Daarnaast beschik je over:
 een afgeronde mbo-opleiding of hoger in de richting van HR of salarisverwerking;
 een PDL-diploma of de bereidheid dit binnen een jaar te behalen;
 actuele kennis van sociale verzekeringen, loonheffingen, pensioen en arbeidsrecht;
 ervaring met SAP of de bereidheid je hierin snel in te werken;
 goede beheersing van de Nederlandse en Engelse taal in woord en geschrift (minimaal niveau B2).

academictransfer:364215.description:
Wil jij leerlingen helpen om hun talenten te ontwikkelen en werkervaring op te doen? Krijg je energie van samenwerken met collega's om een kansrijke leer- en werkomgeving te creëren? Draag als praktijkbegeleider bij aan een inclusieve werkomgeving.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Als praktijkbegeleider ben je samen met je collega verantwoordelijk voor de begeleiding van leerlingen die via Top Academie binnen de UU werkzaam zijn. Samen zorgen jullie ervoor dat iedere leerling de begeleiding krijgt die past bij zijn of haar ontwikkeling.
Je bent regelmatig aanwezig op de werkvloer, onderhoudt contact met leidinggevenden, werkbegeleiders, HR en scholen en bent het eerste aanspreekpunt wanneer er vragen of knelpunten zijn. Daarnaast denk je mee over de verdere ontwikkeling van Top Academie en draag je bij aan de verdere groei en professionalisering van het programma.
 Je werkzaamheden 
Je:
 begeleidt leerlingen met een doelgroepregistratie tijdens hun leer- en werktraject;
 ondersteunt hen bij hun persoonlijke en professionele ontwikkeling;
 voert intake-, voortgangs- en evaluatiegesprekken;
 bewaakt de kwaliteit van de begeleiding;
 signaleert knelpunten en zoekt samen met betrokkenen naar passende oplossingen;
 werkt nauw samen met leidinggevenden, werkbegeleiders, HR en onderwijsinstellingen;
 adviseert afdelingen over passende begeleiding en werkzaamheden;
 draagt bij aan de verdere ontwikkeling van Top Academie;
 helpt mee om nieuwe leer- en werkplekken binnen Campus & Facilities te realiseren;bouwt samen met je collega verder aan een stevig en toekomstbestendig programma.
Je werkt binnen het cluster Staf Bedrijfsvoering van de directie Campus & Facilities in een betrokken team waarin samenwerking en ontwikkeling centraal staan.
Top Academie biedt leerlingen met een doelgroepregistratie de mogelijkheid om binnen de Univeristeit Utrecht werkervaring op te doen en zich voor te bereiden op een duurzame plek op de arbeidsmarkt. Door goede begeleiding en passende werkzaamheden krijgen zij de kans om hun talenten te ontwikkelen en werkervaring op te doen in een veilige en stimulerende omgeving.
Het voormalige programma is inmiddels bijna een jaar geleden ondergebracht in de lijnorganisatie van Campus & Facilities. Sindsdien groeit het aantal aanvragen vanuit de organisatie gestaag. Steeds meer afdelingen zien de meerwaarde van Top Academie en willen leerlingen een plek bieden. Dit betekent dat de werkzaamheden vraagt om uitbreiding van het team met een tweede praktijkbegeleider nodig is

REQUIREMENTS:
Je vindt het belangrijk dat iedereen de kans krijgt zich te ontwikkelen. Je bent geduldig, kunt goed luisteren en weet anderen te enthousiasmeren.
Ervaring met het begeleiden van leerlingen met een doelgroepregistratie is een pré. Heb je die ervaring nog niet, maar spreekt deze doelgroep je aan en wil je je hierin graag verder ontwikkelen? Dan nodigen we je van harte uit om te solliciteren. We vinden jouw motivatie en de bereidheid om te leren minstens zo belangrijk.
 Daarnaast beschik je over: 
 een afgeronde MBO-4 opleiding praktijkbegeleider/leercoach;
 coachende vaardigheden;
 uitstekende communicatieve vaardigheden;
 organisatiesensitiviteit;
 goede plannings- en organisatievaardigheden;
 het vermogen om zelfstandig te werken én prettig samen te werken met collega's.
 Ervaring binnen het onderwijs, het sociaal domein of een grote maatschappelijke organisatie is een pre.

academictransfer:364250.description:
Are you a motivated and proactive colleague who would like to join our Shared Research Facilities (SRF) team in Wageningen and contribute to further developing our research facilities strategy, with the aim of creating greater impact and value through our advanced equipment and research facilities?
Advanced research facilities are an essential part of Wageningen University & Research’s (WUR) innovation and value creation strategy, enabling us to develop solutions that have an impact on the challenges our world faces. Innovative, state-of-the-art research equipment and facilities are essential in order to conduct world-class research into healthy food and a sustainable living environment. Our team is positioned within the Value Creation department and is responsible for coordinating investments in research infrastructure, optimising collaboration and creating value. To achieve this, we make research facilities more visible and encourage the sharing of research facilities both within our organisation and with external parties, many of which are also located on Wageningen Campus. In doing so, the colleagues in the SRF team work closely with colleagues from the Wageningen Campus team.
We are looking for an enterprising Programme Manager who, as part of the SRF team, will work both independently and as part of a team to develop strategic WUR policies for the further development of (shared) research facilities and to realise substantial investments in advanced research equipment. In addition, the Programme Manager will act as Secretary to the SRF Scientific Advisory Board and as Secretary to the SRF Steering Committee.
Being part of the SRF team means being at the heart of innovation processes involving a wide range of people and organisations. In addition to colleagues from Wageningen Campus, internal stakeholders include professors, researchers and management teams from WUR’s five Science Groups, as well as colleagues from Finance & Control, IT and Procurement. There is also extensive interaction with external organisations, including other universities, businesses (including start-ups and other SMEs), public sector organisations such as the Province of Gelderland and the Ministries of Economic Affairs and Climate Policy and Agriculture, Fisheries, Food Security and Nature, as well as organisations such as Foodvalley and Oost NL.
 The role of Programme Manager SRF involves: 
 secretary role: Acting as General Secretary to the SRF Scientific Advisory Board (SAB) and General Secretary to the SRF Steering Committee, providing administrative, communicational and organisational support within the SAB and Steering Committee. Initiating and coordinating both internal and external advisory processes and strategic decision-making regarding the funding of research facilities;
 coordinating and shaping the process from initial idea to the realisation of research facilities within WUR, involving and connecting all relevant stakeholders and playing an active role in strategically developing and deploying the SRF Roadmap;
 further developing our network of (shared) research facilities with both internal and external partners;
 acting as the point of contact for the sharing of research facilities for both internal and external stakeholders;
 grant implementation: Initiating and coordinating investments in shared research equipment and facilities, communicating and reporting on progress to funding bodies, and monitoring the impact of shared research equipment;
 exploring additional funding opportunities and independently preparing and submitting new grant applications. Experience with grant application processes is a plus.
 co-developing and organising meetings and communication activities relating to SRF;
 working together with SRF and Wageningen Campus colleagues to develop a long-term strategy for WUR and the Wageningen Campus ecosystem, with the aim of generating maximum impact and value from research facilities.

REQUIREMENTS:
For this position, we are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic Programme Manager who: 
 is a team player who operates at an academic level and is familiar with the WUR domain;
 has at least five years of recent relevant professional experience and is well acquainted with R&D and the facilities required to support it;
 has excellent written and verbal communication skills, in Dutch (minimum C1 level, hence fluent in Dutch is mandatory) and English;
 can represent WUR internally and externally at various levels;
 has excellent relationship management skills;
 is proactive and flexible;
 combines strong organisational skills with interpersonal sensitivity.

academictransfer:364259.description:
As an experienced Senior Marine Ecologist at Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) in Yerseke, you will lead a team of dedicated researchers who are working together on a range of ecologically relevant issues of societal importance in the South-Western Delta.
As a Senior Marine Ecologist, specialized in benthic organisms and shellfish, you will use your knowledge and experience to carry out research in the South-Western Delta. You will lead a team of enthusiastic fellow ecologists with whom you will carry out research focusing on the effects of human activities such as fishing and sand replenishment, and the consequences of climate change on marine benthic fauna. You will contribute to nature conservation through the active restoration of shellfish beds in the North Sea and the Delta. You have an affinity for applied research and working in complex policy and stakeholder environments. You will initiate new research, including securing funding for it, and lead major projects.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 to act as a leading figure in the field of benthic ecology in contacts with (potential) clients and the media;
 to manage and carry out large-scale and complex research projects;
 securing new research and drafting multidisciplinary research proposals;
 maintaining and expanding the national and international network in the field of delta- and benthic ecology and the restoration of shellfish beds;
 presenting the research findings to the business community, sector organizations, civil society organizations, government bodies and fellow scientists.
 Your team 
Based at WMR’s site in Yerseke, you will work alongside various project teams of differing compositions. As a project leader, you will provide substantive and project-related guidance to field staff, junior and mid-level researchers, enabling them to carry out their work efficiently and thoroughly.
 You will work here 
 Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is one of the nine institutes that form part of Wageningen Research. WMR is a leading, independent research institute with sites in Yerseke, IJmuiden and Den Helder, which focuses on strategic and applied marine ecological research. Products and services are delivered on a project basis and are diverse in nature: field research, realistic-scale experiments, exploratory laboratory-scale studies, data management and modelling. The institute has modern research facilities and is ISO 9001 certified.
Your area of work will primarily be the south-western Delta, but other parts of the Netherlands, such as the North Sea and the Wadden Sea, as well as international projects, are also possibilities.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are proactive, results-oriented and have excellent communication and organizational skills. You are able to maintain an overview of complex processes, work with precision and feel at ease in an environment where different interests and disciplines come together.
 You also possess: 
 relevant university degree and PhD in Marine Ecology;
 extensive work experience as a researcher and project leader in the field of benthic ecology;
 entrepreneurial spirit, decisiveness, the ability to take the initiative, good teamwork skills, and a customer- and results-oriented approach;
 a good command of the Dutch language is a strong advantage.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level B2. If you haven’t yet reached this level, we will of course help you, and you are welcome to visit our language centre, Wageningen in’to Language .

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:364260.description:
Grasslands cover large areas in Europe and foster potential to provide multiple functions, such as ecosystem services. The Horizon Europe GrassConnect project aims to study the effects of farm management on ecosystem service provision, biodiversity, grassland productivity, cultural value, and people’s livelihoods.
GrassConnect is a recently granted, interdisciplinary, and multi-actor European project with 31 partners in 14 countries. GrassConnect will address challenges derived from grassland intensification, by assessing multifunctionality strategies at field, farm and supply-chain level, and aid decision-making to increase the provision of ecosystem services from grasslands.
Within this project, we offer a PhD position ‘Exploring grassland multifunctionality through modelling ’ to study the effects of management in grass-based farms in the Netherlands on biodiversity. This PhD position will focus on grass-based production systems in the Netherlands, whereas GrassConnect will offer the opportunity to engage with other partners covering different grassland systems, production systems and pedo-climatic conditions. Some farm data is available from past and ongoing projects.
After searching, selecting, and analyzing, the data will be used to assess the effects of management on biodiversity and ecosystem services applying modelling approaches from the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. The candidate will be based on the campus of Wageningen University & Research.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 Develop a Research Proposal and a Training and Supervision Plan, to structure the 4-year PhD trajectory;
 Master the state of the art in ecosystem services delivery from grasslands under diverse management regimes;
 Investigate mechanistic relationships between grassland management, ecosystem services and livestock productivity (e.g. regression analysis, mechanistic modelling, meta-analysis);
 Assess environmental and socioeconomic performance of dairy farms under diverse scenarios using a bio-economic optimization model (Dairy LP);
 Disseminate the research results by writing papers and presenting your work at international conferences.
 You will work here 
This PhD will be situated at the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. Here we analyze and explore the roles of livestock in food systems with the aim of making these food systems more sustainable. The group focuses on unravelling the complexity of livestock systems and exploring trade-offs and synergies of innovations regarding environmental impact, animal welfare, and economic viability, to design a more sustainable future.
This PhD project is at the interface of biodiversity and modelling domains within the Animal Production Systems group. You will work under the supervision of Dr. Raimon Ripoll-Bosch and Dr. Aart van der Linden . Raimon Ripoll-Bosch focuses on understanding the complex interactions between livestock, nature (biodiversity and ecosystem services) and economy, to design enabling environments for the transitions to nature-inclusive agroecosystems. Aart van der Linden focuses on modelling farm systems with crops and livestock, including ruminant production on grasslands to improve resource use efficiency and reduce environmental impacts.

REQUIREMENTS:
You have strong quantitative and analytical skills and excel in collaborating in large and multi-disciplinary teams. You are a good communicator to both fellow scientists and farmers, open to learn about different types of models, and passionate about grass-based agriculture.
 You also have: 
 Demonstrated acquaintance with dynamic, mechanistic models and/or optimization models;
 Experience with programming in R, GAMS or Python;
 A successfully completed MSc degree in a relevant field, such as Agronomy, Animal Sciences, Plant Science, Environmental Sciences or Integrative Agricultural Sciences
 Affinity with the systems approach and/or interdisciplinary research;
 Excellent writing skills.
For this position, the excellent command of English language (level C1) is a prerequisite. In some cases, internationally recognised certificate may be required.

CONTRACT: 18 months

academictransfer:364275.description:
In this Postdoc position, you will investigate bone remodelling and fracture healing in a rare genetic bone disorder, Osteogenesis Imperfecta (OI), using a human-relevant organ-on-chip model.
OI is an inherited connective tissue disease characterised by low bone mass, increased bone fragility and frequent fractures, often after very minor trauma. OI is caused by mutations in the genes encoding type I collagen, a key structural protein in bone. These mutations lead to abnormal collagen quantity or quality, resulting in thin, porous and poorly organised bone.
People with OI can experience recurrent fractures, deformities of the long bones and spine, short stature, pain and reduced mobility. Current treatments mainly increase bone mass by inhibiting bone resorption. However, these treatments do not sufficiently restore bone quality or fracture healing, and there is currently no curative therapy.
Within this project, you will use the broken-bone-on-chip (BBoC) model to study how human OI bone remodels and heals under controlled in vitro conditions. In the first phase, you will validate and implement the BBoC technology. In the translational phase, you will study how a candidate pharmaceutical compound influences bone remodelling and fracture healing in the OI-BBoC model.
Your work aims to demonstrate that this human organ-on-chip platform can serve as a reliable measurement model to understand OI pathology and predict therapeutic effects on bone remodelling and fracture healing. Ultimately, your research can contribute to the development of better treatments for people with OI.
Your work starts with the biology inside the chip. Using human bone explants and cells, you will optimise the BBoC model for studying bone remodelling, with a particular focus on osteoclasts and their interaction with osteoblasts.
As the model develops, you will:
 enhance and stabilise the presence of osteoclasts in the system and establish reliable osteoblast–osteoclast communication within the chip;
 design and refine culture conditions, loading schemes and biological read-outs to capture key processes of bone resorption and formation;
 implement and validate the BBoC platform within the Amsterdam UMC Bone and Calcium Unit, using bone tissue from people with OI and healthy donors;
 characterise fracture healing and bone turnover by analysing cell migration, matrix deposition and collagen I remodelling, alongside soluble markers of bone formation and resorption;
 design and perform preclinical experiments with a pharmaceutical compound provided by our industrial partner, Azafaros, including dose-finding, longitudinal read-outs and comparison with current clinical standards.
You will repeatedly sample perfusates for markers such as ALP and P1NP and work closely with colleagues on advanced analyses, including proteomic profiling. Once the model is robust and benchmarked in our setting, you will focus on its core translational goal: testing a pharmaceutical compound in the OI-BBoC model.
Throughout the project, you will work closely with clinicians, basic scientists and engineers. You will play an important role in translating a sophisticated organ-on-chip device into a practical human measurement model that can support future OI therapy development.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a candidate who combines strong experimental skills with an interest in bone biology and translational research. You have:
 a PhD in biomedical sciences, bio-engineering, (molecular) medicine or a closely related field;
 solid experience with advanced cell culture; experience with primary human cells and/or bone or other tissue explants is a strong advantage;
 an affinity with bone biology and bone remodelling, including osteoblasts and osteoclasts, or a clear motivation to specialise in this area;
 experience with biological read-outs, such as RT-PCR, imaging, immunocytochemistry or histology; experience with organ-on-chip or microfluidic models is an asset;
 strong experimental planning and troubleshooting skills, with the ability to carefully standardise and document protocols.
You communicate fluently in spoken and written English and work proactively in a multidisciplinary environment. Experience with osteoclast differentiation or assays, co-culture systems or pharmacological testing in complex in vitro models is an additional advantage.

academictransfer:364276.description:
Alzheimer’s disease (AD) is the most common and currently incurable neurodegenerative disorder. It is characterized by the aggregation of tau and Aβ proteins in the brain. However, the neuropathological picture is often more complex than the aggregation of these two proteins alone. More than half of people with AD show additional aggregation of proteins associated with other neurodegenerative disorders, including amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) and Parkinson’s disease.
In this PhD project, you will investigate the mechanisms underlying the emergence of mixed neuropathologies in the brains of people with AD. You will work at the intersection of different neurodegenerative diseases and use human autopsy brain tissue, transcriptomics and induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-based cell culture models.
Your research will contribute to identifying disease mechanisms in AD, with potential relevance to other neurodegenerative disorders. During this four-year PhD project, you will work closely with molecular neuroscientists, neuropathology experts and bioinformaticians
 Would you like to know more about the different phases within the PhD trajectory? You can read more about this on this page . 
You will investigate the biological mechanisms underlying mixed neuropathologies in Alzheimer’s disease. Your work combines human brain tissue research with advanced molecular and cellular models. You will:
 perform cutting-edge transcriptomic analyses;
 work with iPSC-based neural cultures and develop novel disease models;
 work with human autopsy brain tissue from people with Alzheimer’s disease, strengthening the translational impact of your research;
 study biological mechanisms in Alzheimer’s disease and identify potential therapeutic targets.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a motivated researcher with an interest in neurodegenerative diseases and neuropathology. You have:
 a Master’s degree in neuroscience, biomedical sciences, biology or a related field;
 a demonstrable interest in neurodegenerative diseases and neuropathology;
 a strong background in wet-lab research; experience with cell culture is an advantage;
 strong analytical skills; experience with bioinformatics is an advantage;
 good oral and written communication skills in English.

academictransfer:364281.description:
Werk je graag samen met collega’s om het bestuur en management binnen de faculteit optimaal te faciliteren? De faculteit Geowetenschappen zoekt een management- en office-assistent (MOA) voor de afdeling Bestuurs- en Beleidsondersteuning.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Je werkt binnen de afdeling Bestuurs- en Beleidsondersteuning (BBO) samen met vier collega’s, onder leiding van de bestuurssecretaris van de faculteit. Samen met de collega’s van team BBO bied je ondersteuning aan de decaan, twee vice-decanen, de faculteitsdirecteur, de hoofden van het faculteitsbureau, de faculteitsraad en diverse commissies en overleggen. Ieder heeft een eigen takenpakket, waarbij jullie elkaar kunnen vervangen en helpen bij werkpieken en afwezigheid.
Je hoofdtaak is het bieden van ondersteuning aan de faculteitsdirecteur en het managementteam. Je werkzaamheden bestaan bijvoorbeeld uit:
 complex agendabeheer;
 notuleren van complexe vergaderingen, waarbij zowel Nederlands als Engels wordt gesproken;
 plannen en organiseren van bijeenkomsten en overleggen;
 beheren van teamsites en andere fysieke en/of cloud-omgevingen;
 administratieve ondersteuning bij oa. HR-processen;
 algemene secretariële werkzaamheden als bestellingen, ontvangen bezoekers, secretariaatsmailbox, (mede) organiseren facultaire bijeenkomsten.

REQUIREMENTS:
Jij bent een breed inzetbare en ervaren management- en office-assistent die gericht is op samenwerking. Je hebt een proactieve en positieve instelling. Je bent iemand aan wie je graag iets vraagt en bij wie je erop kunt vertrouwen dat het in orde komt. Je past goed in deze functie als je:
 beschikt over een relevante werkervaring in een vergelijkbare omgeving;
 gericht bent op samenwerken en daarnaast ook goed zelfstandig kunt werken, je eigen werk in plant en organiseert;
 gevoel hebt voor service en voor bestuurlijke verhoudingen en weet om te gaan met vertrouwelijke informatie;
 accuraat werkt en betrouwbaar bent;
 een goede beheersing hebt van het Nederlands en Engels;
 vaardig bent met softwarepakketten (Office-programma’s) en in staat bent om je de administratieve systemen van de universiteit snel eigen te maken;
 graag wilt leren en je persoonlijk en professioneel wilt blijven ontwikkelen;
 affiniteit hebt met de universitaire omgeving en geïnteresseerd bent in de aarde, klimaat en duurzaamheid.

academictransfer:364293.description:
Wil je bijdragen aan de opleiding van toekomstige orthopedagogen en aan onderzoek naar ontwikkeling, opvoeding en ondersteuning? Als universitair docent Orthopedagogiek combineer je onderwijs en onderzoek en breng je expertise mee die relevant is voor het klinische domein van de orthopedagogiek. We staan open voor uiteenlopende inhoudelijke specialisaties, doelgroepen en onderzoeksperspectieven.
 Wat ga je doen? 
Als universitair docent lever je een belangrijke bijdrage aan ons onderwijs en onderzoek op het terrein van de orthopedagogiek. Je houdt je bezig met vraagstukken rond de ontwikkeling en het functioneren van kinderen en jongeren in de context waarin zij opgroeien, zoals het gezin, de school en de bredere maatschappelijke context, en met de ondersteuning van kinderen, jongeren en gezinnen wanneer de ontwikkeling onder druk staat.
En verder:
 verzorg je onderwijs binnen de bachelor Pedagogische Wetenschappen en de master Orthopedagogiek;
 draag je bij aan onderwijs op het gebied van bijvoorbeeld ontwikkelingspsychopathologie, diagnostiek, behandeling en interventie, evidence-based werken en professioneel handelen;
 begeleid je studenten bij hun bachelor- en masterthesis en kunt een rol vervullen in de begeleiding van studenten tijdens hun stage;
 draag je bij aan de coördinatie en verdere ontwikkeling van het onderwijs.
In je onderzoek richt je je op vraagstukken die relevant zijn voor de orthopedagogiek. We staan daarbij nadrukkelijk open voor verschillende inhoudelijke specialisaties en onderzoeksperspectieven. Je onderzoek kan zich bijvoorbeeld richten op ontwikkeling en psychopathologie, opvoeding en gezin, diagnostiek, preventie, interventie, of op de professionele en maatschappelijke context waarin ondersteuning plaatsvindt. Daarbij kan je onderzoek zich richten op uiteenlopende doelgroepen, problematieken en contexten.
Kenmerkend voor je werk is dat je wetenschappelijke en klinische perspectieven met elkaar verbindt:
 je draagt bij aan onderzoek dat niet alleen theoretisch relevant is, maar ook betekenis heeft voor de professionele praktijk;
 je zoekt daarbij waar mogelijk samenwerking met collega’s binnen en buiten de universiteit en met partners in het klinische en maatschappelijke werkveld.

REQUIREMENTS:
je herkent jezelf in het volgende profiel:
 je bent gepromoveerd, of rondt op korte termijn je promotie af, binnen de Orthopedagogiek, (Klinische) Ontwikkelingspsychologie of een nauw verwant vakgebied;
 je beschikt over brede kennis van klinisch-orthopedagogische vraagstukken rond kinderen en jongeren en de context waarin zijn opgroeien;
 je hebt aantoonbare affiniteit met de klinische praktijk (in ieder geval op het gebied van onderzoek; klinische praktijk ervaring en/of registratie strekt tot aanbeveling);
 je hebt ervaring met of duidelijke belangstelling voor onderwijs op het gebied van bijvoorbeeld diagnostiek, ontwikkelingspsychopathologie, interventie en behandeling en/of klinische professionele vaardigheden;
 je hebt ervaring met het opzetten, uitvoeren en publiceren van wetenschappelijk onderzoek en hebt de ambitie een eigen, herkenbare onderzoekslijn verder te ontwikkelen;
 je bent bereid om bij te dragen aan het verwerven van externe onderzoeksmiddelen;
 je werkt graag samen met maatschappelijke en klinische partners;
 je bent een enthousiaste en betrokken docent en begeleider van studenten en draagt graag bij aan de ontwikkeling van het onderwijs;
 je werkt graag samen en draagt bij aan een open en collegiale academische werkomgeving;
 je hebt een goede beheersing van het Nederlands en Engels, of de bereidheid om binnen een afgesproken termijn voldoende beheersing van het Nederlands te verwerven;
 je hebt een Basiskwalificatie Onderwijs (BKO), of de bereidheid deze binnen afzienbare tijd te behalen.

academictransfer:364294.description:
Wij zijn op zoek naar drie nieuwe gepromoveerde junior collega’s die breed inzetbaar zijn binnen ons onderwijskundig advies- en professionaliseringsaanbod en zich hierop willen ontwikkelen. Kennis over of ervaring met de specifieke onderwerpen interactie in het onderwijs, differentiatie, motivatie of ontwerp van onderwijs is een pre.
Vertaal jij met jouw advies en trainingen actuele onderwijskundige inzichten naar de praktijk? Dan komen we graag met je in contact!
 Wat ga je doen? 
Als onderwijskundig adviseur/trainer bij Onderwijsadvies en Training (O&T) geef je trainingen aan onderwijsprofessionals en begeleid je bij onderwijsontwikkeling. Dit doe je binnen het hoger onderwijs (zowel binnen de Universiteit Utrecht als bij andere universiteiten) en in andere onderwijssectoren zoals PO en VO. Je levert veelal maatwerk gebaseerd op actuele wetenschappelijk inzichten. Je hebt autonomie in de uitvoering van dit werk en kan tegelijk op collegiale steun en samenwerking rekenen.
De onderwijsprofessionals waar je mee werkt zijn onder andere universitair (hoofd)docenten, professoren, onderwijsdirecteuren, (vice-)decanen, schoolleiders, leraren en beleidsmakers.

REQUIREMENTS:
Als kandidaat voor deze functie heb je goede communicatieve vaardigheden in het Nederlands én het Engels en werk je vanuit een onderbouwde visie op goed onderwijs. Daarnaast vragen we in het ideale geval het volgende:
 Je bent gepromoveerd in één van de onderwijswetenschappen of in een andere wetenschap gecombineerd met een stevige onderwijskundige expertise en ervaring;
 Je hebt ervaring met of affiniteit met advies en trainingen voor onderwijsprofessionals,;
 Je kunt een gesprekspartner zijn voor docenten en leidinggevenden in het onderwijs en kunt met hen meedenken;
 Je kunt zowel zelfstandig werken als goed samenwerken;
 Je hebt, of je werkt aan een netwerk in het hoger, voortgezet, beroeps- of primair onderwijs;
 Je neemt initiatief en bent vernieuwingsgezind;
Als je denkt dat je niet aan al deze kwalificaties voldoet, maar dat je toch goed in ons team zou passen, ook dan nodigen we je graag uit om te reageren.
Iedereen verdient het om zich thuis te voelen bij onze universiteit. We verwelkomen medewerkers met een grote verscheidenheid aan achtergronden en perspectieven.

academictransfer:364298.description:
Are you motivated to advance operational forest disturbance monitoring using radar satellite data and to contribute to global efforts to understand and combat forest loss? Then this positions might be your perfect next step! 
We are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to advance near-real-time forest disturbance and regrowth monitoring using radar satellite observations. The position combines methodological research with the further development and operation of the RADD (Radar for Detecting Deforestation) forest disturbance alerts , using established and newly available radar satellite missions, including Sentinel-1 and NISAR.
Radar remote sensing is particularly valuable for forest monitoring because of its sensitivity to vegetation structure and its ability to provide systematic observations largely independent of daylight and cloud cover. Translating these observations into accurate, consistent, and operational information on forest disturbance and post-disturbance regrowth, however, remains challenging. Radar responses are influenced by wavelength, acquisition geometry, forest structure, seasonality, and environmental conditions.
The project will address these challenges in two main directions. First, you will extend the RADD framework from disturbance detection towards continuous forest change monitoring, including the detection and characterization of post-disturbance forest regrowth and structural recovery. Second, you will expand the geographic applicability of the RADD alerts beyond their current implementations in the humid tropics and Europe.
The position is embedded in a strong international and interdisciplinary research environment, including collaboration with partners from Global Nature Watch. The project provides opportunities to contribute to high-impact scientific publications, international collaborations, and operational monitoring products with direct societal relevance.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 operational lead of the RADD near-real-time alert system;
 developing and implementing methods for radar-based methods for detecting and monitoring post-disturbance forest regrowth and structural recovery;
 studying forest disturbance-recovery dynamics;
 coordinating project deliverables and ensuring their timely and high-quality completion;
 contributing to collaborative research in an international consortium;
 publishing research results in leading peer-reviewed journals;
 communicating research findings and monitoring developments to scientific, policy, and stakeholder audiences.
 Your team 
You will join the radar remote sensing team within the Laboratory of Geo-information Science and Remote Sensing . The team studies human activities and the dynamics of forest ecosystems from regional to global scales using radar remote sensing. Its work focuses in particular on fundamental radar and multi-sensor methods, near-real-time change monitoring, and the integration of satellite observations with in-situ field data.
 You will work here 
You will work as part of a research team within the Laboratory of Geo-Information Science and Remote Sensing (GRS), which is led by Dr. Johannes Reiche .

REQUIREMENTS:
You are a highly motivated and independent researcher with strong methodological skills and demonstrated expertise in (radar) remote sensing. You have a clear interest in multidisciplinary research at the interface of remote sensing, forest ecology, and forest management.
You combine scientific rigor with the ability to work collaboratively in an international research environment and to translate methodological advances into robust monitoring approaches.
 You also possess: 
 a PhD in remote sensing, data science, forest ecology or a related field;
 a strong foundation in remote sensing, including demonstrated experience with radar/SAR remote sensing;
 experience with forest change, disturbance, regrowth, or recovery dynamics;
 experience developing reproducible data-processing and analysis workflows;
 excellent programming skills, with strong proficiency in Python;
 the ability to publish scientific research and communicate results effectively;
 experience working in interdisciplinary teams and with stakeholders;
 excellent written and spoken English skills (C1 level).
 Preferred Qualifications: 
The following qualifications are considered an advantage:
 experience with satellite time-series analysis, change detection and near-real-time forest monitoring;
 experience with Sentinel-1, NISAR, and/or other spaceborne radar missions;
 experience with scalable Earth observation processing using cloud-computing or high-performance computing environments;
 knowledge of forest disturbance ecology, forest management, and post-disturbance vegetation dynamics;
 experience engaging with operational users, policy actors, or other stakeholders.

CONTRACT: 2 years

academictransfer:364327.description:
In Huntington’s Disease (HD), expansion of CAG repeats in the huntingtin (HTT) gene leads to a toxic gain-of-function that ultimately results in fatal neurodegeneration. HD research has long focused on the toxic effects of the HTT-encoded polyglutamine protein that aggregates in neurons. However, recent discoveries reveal that the HTT mRNA itself forms condensates and aggregates, contributing to neuronal toxicity. In this project, your work will help define the origins of RNA toxicity in HD - a critical step toward developing therapies.
 Your job 
As a PhD candidate in this project, you will design, execute, and analyze single-molecule experiments using optical tweezers and confocal fluorescence imaging, building on previous work (O’Brien et al., Nat Comm 2024). Together with our collaborators, you will produce materials for these experiments and develop novel approaches for protein and RNA labeling. Working at the interface of biophysics and biochemistry, you will establish new paradigms that explain why some repeat RNAs are toxic while others are not.
Your responsibilities include:
 Independently conducting experimental work and performing data analysis;
 Presenting your progress in regular team meetings;
 Publishing your findings in peer-reviewed journals;
 Participating in scientific conferences;
 Supporting teaching and mentoring undergraduate and master's students.
Under the mentorship of Prof. Christian Kaiser, you will have the freedom to explore your own ideas within the project goals. You will work closely with junior investigators in the group as well as with our local and international collaborators, and engage with the broader research community at the Bijvoet Centre and the Faculty of Science. Visit the Kaiser Lab and the Bijvoet Centre websites for more information.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are genuinely curious and passionate about science, motivated to work independently, and eager to understand biology at the molecular and biophysical level. You have strong critical thinking and problem-solving skills, and you bring:
 An MSc degree in (bio)physics, (bio)chemistry, or a related field;
 Experience with wet lab work and data analysis;
 Strong scientific writing and communication skills;
 Proficiency in written and spoken English;
Experience with single-molecule approaches (not required, but a plus).

academictransfer:364345.description:
Ben jij toegankelijk, empathisch en heb je daarnaast de nodige dosis lef? Ben je discreet, onafhankelijk en in staat verschillende belangen te onderscheiden in een grote en complexe organisatie? Kun je in ingewikkelde en vertrouwelijke situaties opereren? Houd je ervan om mensen te helpen?
 Wat ga je doen? 
De faculteit Geowetenschappen vindt een veilig sociaal klimaat voor alle studenten en medewerkers belangrijk. We behandelen elkaar met respect. Signalen van ongewenst gedrag zoals (seksuele) intimidatie, discriminatie, agressie, geweld of pesten, worden serieus genomen. De Universiteit heeft daarvoor een zorgstructuur ingericht. Op universitair niveau zijn er vertrouwenspersonen, ombudspersonen en de Commissie Interpersoonlijke Integriteit. De faculteiten hebben een Contactpersoon Ongewenst gedrag die medewerkers en/ of studenten informeert waar ze terecht kunnen met hun vragen. Keuzehulp - In vertrouwen praten, melden of een klacht indienen - Universiteit Utrecht 
In aanvulling hierop heeft de faculteit Geowetenschappen een facultaire vertrouwenspersoon voor de in- en externe facultaire promovendi. Vanwege overstap van de huidige vertrouwenspersoon binnen de UU zoeken wij daarom nu een nieuwe vertrouwenspersoon voor PhDs en postdocs. De vertrouwenspersoon fungeert als eerste aanspreekpunt voor alle promovendi en postdocs van de Faculteit Geowetenschappen die klachten of vragen hebben over de omstandigheden of gebeurtenissen op het werk. Dit kan verband houden met de leidinggevende/promotor, collega’s, de werksfeer of de manier waarop met klachten of vragen is omgegaan. Als vertrouwenspersoon ben je een laagdrempelige gesprekspartner voor de promovendus/postdoc. Je functioneert zonder last of ruggespraak, staat daarbij naast de promovendus/postdoc en waarborgt zorgvuldigheid en vertrouwelijkheid. Je rapporteert inhoudelijk doch anoniem/vertrouwelijk aan het faculteitsbestuur, waarbij de vice-decaan onderzoek je bestuurlijk portefeuillehouder is, en de faculteitsdirecteur je hiërarchisch leidinggevende.
 Wat ga je doen? 
 Je informeert en adviseert promovendi en postdocs over mogelijke oplossingsrichtingen of te nemen stappen en biedt desgewenst verdere ondersteuning bijvoorbeeld bij het indienen van een melding als dat aan de orde is of bij het aangaan van gesprekken;
 Je houdt preventieve gesprekken met (nieuwe) promovendi en postdocs;
 Je geeft Faculteitsbestuur, onderzoeksdirecteuren, programmaleider onderwijs van de Graduate school en overige belanghebbenden gevraagd en ongevraagd advies;
 Je registreert meldingen en brengt jaarlijks een geanonimiseerd verslag uit aan het Faculteitsbestuur;
 Je zorgt voor voorlichting en publiciteit;
 Je maakt deel uit van het universitaire collegiale Zorgstructuuroverleg.
Afhankelijk van ontwikkelingen binnen de UU zijn wijzigingen in de exacte focus en in de inbedding van de functie op termijn mogelijk.
Om absolute onafhankelijkheid en vertrouwelijkheid te waarborgen zoeken wij een UU-kandidaat buiten de faculteit Geowetenschappen.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je hebt een afgeronde WO- of HBO-opleiding;
 Je bent bekend met de Nederlandse academische wereld; hebt gevoel voor en ervaring met het werken in een internationale en cultureel diverse werkomgeving. Kennis van de UU en ervaring met het doen van promotie-onderzoek is een pre;
 Je hebt een opleiding tot vertrouwenspersoon (Landelijke Vereniging van Vertrouwenspersonen) of bent bereid deze te volgen en te zorgen voor registratie bij de LVV;
 Je hebt een goed gevoel voor gedrag van mensen, voor verschillen tussen culturen en generaties, voor onderlinge verhoudingen en voor organisatiecultuur;
 Je bent door je reflectievermogen en heldere feedback in staat oordeelvrij door te vragen naar het doel van de medewerker en de wegen die diegene zou willen bewandelen;
 Je weet afstand te houden tot de casuïstiek;
 Je houdt je nauwgezet aan sociale, ethische en professionele normen en waarden, onder andere verwoord in onze Code of Conduct en de cao Nederlandse Universiteiten;
 Verder heb je, gezien het zeer internationale karakter van onze faculteit, uitstekende mondelinge en schriftelijke vaardigheden in zowel het Nederlands als het Engels.

academictransfer:364357.description:
Looking for a PhD position to contribute to environmental justice in Brazil’s Cerrado? Join an interdisciplinary research team exploring how small-scale farmers and traditional communities can foster nature conservation, sustainable production and territorial justice. You will collaborate with researchers, producers, practitioners, and grassroots organizations to co-produce knowledge that supports a resilient Cerrado and sustainable sociobioeconomies in a region facing rapid agribusiness expansion and climate change.
 Strengthening Justice and Resilience through Sociobioeconomies in Brazil’s Cerrado 
The CERRADO Project—Conservation and Ecological Restoration for Resilient Agriculture and Development Outcomes —examines how agricultural development and biodiversity conservation can be better balanced in Brazil’s Cerrado, particularly in the rapidly transforming MATOPIBA region. It addresses pressures arising from agribusiness expansion, deforestation, land conflicts, and increasingly severe climate impacts, while exploring how sustainable practices developed by Indigenous peoples, traditional communities, and family farmers can contribute to sustainability and agrarian justice in the region.
The PhD research will contribute to the assessment of the sociobiodiversity economy in the MATOPIBA region, including small-scale farming, agroforestry, and sustainable extraction, as well as to in-depth socioeconomic and governance analyses of two cases involving local producers living in private and collective settlements in southern Maranhão. The regional assessment will examine the diversity, extent, challenges and social-economic relevance of sociobiodiversity production systems across MATOPIBA, as well as identify the key social, epistemic and political factors shaping these production systems within landscapes increasingly dominated by large-scale agriculture. The case studies will focus on producers’ social relations and organization, knowledge systems, governance arrangements, economic conditions, political mobilization, and perceptions of agrarian transformations and climate change. They will also examine how these production systems, in turn, contribute to the maintenance of natural systems and local livelihoods.
The PhD research will be carried out in collaboration with local stakeholders and other project members and will be closely aligned with the project’s other two pillars: ecological assessment and advocacy . The ecological assessment includes an integrated socioecological analysis of the contributions of different production systems to biodiversity conservation, water regulation, carbon storage, pollination, pest control, local livelihoods, food security, and climate resilience. The advocacy pillar will focus on translating research findings into policy recommendations and other societal products, thereby strengthening the contribution of the research to sustainable development and agrarian justice in the region.
 This is what you will be doing 
 Developing skills for academic training and participatory research 
 submission of a PhD thesis within the period of appointment;
 completion and submission of three article manuscripts to peer-reviewed journals within the period of appointment;
 presenting intermediate research results at workshops and conferences;
 participation in the ARTES Research School and Faculty of Humanities PhD training programmes;
 (co-)teaching courses at BA-level in the second and third year of the appointment (maximum 0,2 fte per year, which corresponds to approximately 8 hours per week);
 participating in meetings of the project research group and developing a shared database;
 provide support to organization and execution of project-related activities;
 co-supervise two Master research theses in your PhD research area;
 participation in national and international academic events;
 organising knowledge dissemination activities;
 participating actively in meetings of the project research team and developing a shared database and eventually co-publications.
 This is what we ask of you 
 a completed Master's degree in Social Sciences or Environmental Sciences; preferably with focus on socioenvironmental and agrarian change;
 excellent research skills demonstrated by a Master thesis and/or published articles;
 demonstrable knowledge in theoretical perspectives relevant to environmental and agrarian justice;
 solid knowledge of qualitative research methods;
 research experience with rural communities in Brazil;
 experience with interdisciplinary research and collaboration with non-academic actors is desirable;
 excellent spoken Portuguese, and language proficiency in English;
 availability for extended fieldwork stays in remote areas at time.
 Please note that if you already hold a doctorate/PhD or are working towards obtaining a similar degree elsewhere, you will not be admitted to a doctoral programme at the UvA. 
