academictransfer:364369.description:
De werkzaamheden 
Het Instituut Biologie Leiden (IBL) verwelkomt jaarlijks circa 140 tot 200 eerstejaarsstudenten Biologie. Tijdens hun eerste studiejaar volgen zij een gevarieerd programma met hoor- en werkcolleges, excursies en practica. Dit onderwijs wordt verzorgd door een enthousiast team van onderzoekers, docenten, studentenassistenten en ondersteunend personeel.
Als practicumondersteuner speel je een belangrijke rol in het onderwijs aan eerstejaarsstudenten. Je bent betrokken bij de voorbereiding, uitvoering en ondersteuning van practica binnen uiteenlopende vakgebieden, variërend van microbiologie en celbiologie tot biodiversiteit en de biologie van planten en dieren.
Je werkzaamheden bestaan onder meer uit:
 Voorbereiden van practica, waaronder het maken van media en oplossingen;
 Reinigen, steriliseren en op voorraad houden van materialen en glaswerk;
 Verzamelen, voorbereiden en klaarzetten van biologische preparaten;
 Opruimen en schoonhouden van practicumzalen en materialen;
 Verantwoord afvoeren van chemisch en microbiologisch afval;
 Toezien op een veilige werkomgeving voor studenten en medewerkers;
 Beheren en onderhouden van apparatuur, zoals microscopen en andere onderwijsinstrumenten;
 Signaleren van storingen en gebreken aan apparatuur en het doen van voorstellen voor reparatie of vervanging;
 Samenwerken met docenten, studentenassistenten en collega's van de ondersteunende diensten om kwalitatief hoogwaardig onderwijs mogelijk te maken.
 Beschrijving faculteit / directie 
De faculteit der Wiskunde en Natuurwetenschappen is een toonaangevende faculteit waarin medewerkers en studenten werken in een dynamische, internationale omgeving en waar persoonlijke en academische ontwikkeling hoog in het vaandel staan. Onze mensen worden geïnspireerd door nieuwsgierigheid, om zo fundamentele kennis te vergroten en over grenzen heen te kijken. Voor de wetenschap, én om een bijdrage te leveren aan grote maatschappelijke uitdagingen van morgen.
Het onderzoek binnen de faculteit der Wiskunde en Natuurwetenschappen varieert van wiskunde, informatica, sterrenkunde, natuurkunde, scheikunde en biofarmaceutische wetenschappen tot aan biologie en milieuwetenschappen en is belegd bij acht instituten. Die verzorgen tevens acht bachelor- en twaalf masteropleidingen. De faculteit is de afgelopen jaren sterk gegroeid en telt meer dan 2300 medewerkers en bijna 5000 studenten. We zijn gesitueerd in het hart van het Bio Science Park, één van Europa’s grootste scienceparken, waar universiteit en bedrijfsleven elkaar ontmoeten.
 Het Instituut voor Biologie 
Het onderzoek binnen het Instituut Biologie Leiden (IBL) heeft tot doel te werken aan de wetenschappelijke basis van biodiversiteit en gezondheid. Dit zie je terug in ons leidende principe Biodiversity and Health . We voeren innovatief onderzoek uit om fundamentele vragen te beantwoorden en oplossingsgericht onderzoek om grote maatschappelijke uitdagingen op te lossen. Denk aan het beschermen van de biodiversiteit van de natuur, ontwikkelen van duurzame biotechnologie en landbouw en verhogen van een goede gezondheid. Ons onderzoek richt zich op vier onderzoeksthema's: Bioactive Molecules, Host-Microbe Interactions, Development & Disease and Evolution & Biodiversity. Gelegen in een bloeiende wetenschappelijke omgeving met onze Faculteit Wis- en Natuurwetenschappen, Naturalis Biodiversity Center, het Leids Universitair Medisch Centrum en het Leiden Bioscience Park biedt IBL een boeiende, internationaal georiënteerde en inclusieve plek om te werken en studeren.
 Jouw profiel 
Je bent praktisch ingesteld, werkt nauwkeurig en vindt het leuk om studenten te ondersteunen in een dynamische onderwijsomgeving. Daarnaast beschik je over:
 Een mbo-diploma in een relevante laboratorium- of life sciences-richting;
 Bij voorkeur ervaring met moleculaire, celbiologische en/of biochemische technieken;
 Affiniteit met biodiversiteit, biologische collecties en preparaten van planten, dieren en micro-organismen;
 Ervaring met of interesse in het beheer en onderhoud van laboratoriumapparatuur;
 Een proactieve, zelfstandige en nauwkeurige werkhouding;
 Goede beheersing van de Nederlandse taal;
 Affiniteit met het werken in een onderwijsomgeving en met studenten;
 Een sterk verantwoordelijkheidsgevoel en aandacht voor veiligheid.
 Wij bieden 
Het betreft een tijdelijk dienstverband voor de periode van 1 jaar. Aanstelling vindt plaats conform de cao Nederlandse Universiteiten.
Het salaris bedraagt, afhankelijk van opleiding en werkervaring, minimaal € 3.173,-- en maximaal € 3.785,- bruto per maand bij een fulltime aanstelling (schaal 6).
Daarnaast kent de Universiteit Leiden goede arbeidsvoorwaarden. Zo is er sprake van een eindejaarsuitkering (8,3%), vakantietoeslag (8%), uitstekende opleidingsfaciliteiten, betaald ouderschapsverlof , sabbatical leave en de mogelijkheid om via een keuzemodel arbeidsvoorwaarden op maat te kiezen. Alle informatie hierover kunt u nalezen op: www.werkenbij.le

academictransfer:364375.description:
As a Marine Ecologist specialized in the Delta ecosystems at Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) in Yerseke, you will play an active role in restoring and enhancing the unique natural environment of the Delta waters. Using innovative, sustainable solutions based on the philosophy of ‘building with nature’, you will translate scientific knowledge into practical applications with a tangible impact.
The South-Western Delta presents major ecological challenges as well as opportunities. From the sand deficiency in the Oosterschelde to nature conservation in the Westerschelde and improving water quality in Delta waters such as Lake Grevelingen and Lake Veere: you will make a significant contribution to solutions that make a difference to nature and society.
Together with other experts in the field of marine ecology, you will work on a wide range of delta-related issues. You will investigate where nature development and nature-based solutions can be applied to areas such as flood protection and water quality improvement and will be able to convincingly demonstrate to clients the added value of this sustainable approach. In addition, you will contribute to the scientific basis of the Nature Restoration Plan and the management plans for Natura 2000 sites in the South-Western Delta. Liaising with clients and stakeholders is an integral part of your work.
 Your duties and responsibilities as a Marine Ecologist include: 
 contribute to a wide range of literature reviews, data analyses, and field and laboratory experiments in the field of nature conservation and benthic fauna research;
 assist with securing new projects and drafting research proposals;
 communicate with fellow researchers and clients;
 report and present research findings to clients, attend conferences and take part in workshops;
 publish and present results in academic journals and at conferences.
 Your team 
Based at WMR’s site in Yerseke, you will work alongside various project teams of differing compositions. As a project leader, you will provide substantive and project-related guidance to fellow researchers so that they can carry out their work efficiently and thoroughly. As a project member, you will often focus on a specific area that matches your expertise.
 You will work here 
 Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is one of the nine institutes that form part of Wageningen Research. WMR is a leading, independent research institute with sites in Yerseke, IJmuiden and Den Helder, which focuses on strategic and applied marine ecological research. Products and services are delivered on a project basis and are diverse in nature: field research, realistic-scale experiments, exploratory laboratory-scale studies, data management and modelling. The institute has modern research facilities and is ISO 9001 certified.
Your area of work will primarily be the south-western Delta, but other parts of the Netherlands, such as the North Sea and the Wadden Sea, as well as international projects, are also possibilities.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are curious about the interactions within ecosystems and are committed to contributing to the protection and restoration of marine and coastal ecosystems. Thanks to your ability to take a broader perspective, you feel at home at the interface between science and policy. You naturally build professional relationships with others and value each team member’s contribution to the overall project outcome. You can cope with time pressure and plan your work to meet deadlines. You are driven, proactive, collaborative, customer-focused and a strong communicator.
 You also possess: 
 a successfully completed relevant academic degree in the natural sciences or life sciences. A PhD in the field specified for this post is an advantage;
 experience with nature based solutions for flood protection;
 demonstrable work experience in ecological estuary and Delta research;
 a good command of both Dutch and English.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level B2.

CONTRACT: 1 year

academictransfer:364411.description:
We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher to work on the governance of transitions towards more sustainable and regenerative farming systems. Governance instruments — including certification schemes, private standards, and policy incentives — play an important role in shaping the future of livestock farming systems. Are you curious about how standards, incentives, and sustainability indicators influence farming systems and farmers’ livelihoods? Are you excited to contribute to interdisciplinary research that supports transitions towards more sustainable farming systems? Then we would love to hear from you. 
We are looking for a candidate with experience in the governance of farming systems, who is highly motivated to contribute to more sustainable food systems through inter- and transdisciplinary research. You are a systems thinker with an interest in public-private governance of livestock farming systems, agricultural policy and/or sustainable finance. You are familiar with current policy debates on the future of European and Dutch agriculture and enjoy working at the interface of science, policy and practice.
You will strengthen the team working on farming systems governance by contributing to research on public and private governance instruments, sustainability indicators, and perceptions of those affected by transitions. Your work will include contributing to ongoing research on regenerative agriculture (including ReGeNL), facilitating stakeholder engagement, supporting project management, developing research proposals, and contributing to education. You are encouraged to describe relevant experience with these tasks in your CV and motivation letter.
 You will work here 
This postdoc will be situated at the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. Here we analyze and explore the roles of livestock in food systems with the aim of making these food systems more sustainable. The group focuses on unravelling the complexity of livestock systems, and exploring trade-offs and synergies of innovations regarding environmental impact, animal welfare, and economic viability, to design a more sustainable future.
You will join the team led by Dr. Evelien de Olde and contribute to research on governance issues in farming systems within a polycentric food systems context, including sustainability certification and standards, the role of metrics and KPIs, and the impact of sustainability transitions on livestock farming systems.

REQUIREMENTS:
A completed PhD degree with an interdisciplinary focus in a relevant field such as sustainability science, agricultural science, animal science, political science, environmental science, or related disciplines;
 Curiosity to explore leverage points and complexities in the governance of farming systems while working in transdisciplinary settings with project partners including farmers;
 Affinity with farming systems research, sustainability indicators, and governance theories ;
 Experience with diverse qualitative and/or mixed-method research methods such as interviews, focus groups, serious games, and stakeholder engagement;
 Interest in developing research proposals and contributing to future projects;
 You are a good team player, collaborative and creative who can communicate easily with diverse audiences and act proactively;
 You posses strong communication skills, both verbal and written, in Dutch and English (C1 level);
 You are able and willing to work from Wageningen at least 3 days a week.

CONTRACT: 2 years

academictransfer:364417.description:
Do you enjoy variety in your work, want to contribute to fish and fisheries research, and enjoy collaborating with colleagues? Then we have an exciting opportunity for you at Wageningen Marine Research as a laboratory technician in fish biology! A good command of the Dutch language is essential for this role.
As a laboratory technician in fish biology, you will support fish and fisheries research through your practical experience, species identification skills and accurate working methods. You will help ensure biological samples are processed reliably and that researchers have access to high-quality data.
 Your duties and responsibilities as a Laboratory technician fish biology include: 
 processing biological samples in WMR’s research laboratories;
 determining the age of various fish species, including preparing samples and contributing to the development of fish ageing methods;
 recording research data, identifying and reporting any anomalies, and providing data to researchers;
 archiving and managing biological sample collections and research materials;
 coordinating sampling logistics, both in the laboratory and at field locations throughout the Netherlands.
 Your team 
You will work with colleagues across various fish research project teams. Your work will support a broad range of studies, including larval fish research and diet studies. You will combine taking initiative to improve working methods with carrying out routine laboratory tasks.
 You will work here 
You will be based at Wageningen Marine Research in IJmuiden, working in its offices and research laboratories.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an accurate, proactive and solution-oriented team player with good communication skills. You switch easily between tasks and work effectively both independently and as part of a team. You have a natural aptitude for organising tasks, materials, and samples in a systematic, structured manner.
 You also possess: 
 a Dutch senior secondary vocational qualification at level 4 (MBO-4), or an equivalent qualification, in a biological discipline, and an interest in fish and fisheries research; Applicants with a university degree are excluded from the selection process.
 demonstrable experience in biological laboratory work, preferably in a research environment; experience working with fish and other marine species is an advantage;
 proficiency in Dutch at a minimum of CEFR level B2;
 the attention to detail required to interpret and follow sampling and data protocols accurately;
 preferably, a category B driving licence.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level B1.

CONTRACT: 12 months

academictransfer:364422.description:
Binnen het Onderwijscentrum Diergeneeskunde (OWC) zijn we op zoek naar een enthousiaste ‘alleskunner’ voor ons team Docentondersteuning & Studiepunt. Het OWC is de plek waar we met elkaar werken aan kwalitatieve onderwijsprogramma’s die goed studeerbaar, doceerbaar, organiseerbaar en betaalbaar zijn. We zijn een team dat volop oog heeft voor studenten, docenten, de organisatie en elkaar.
Krijg je energie van het creëren van oplossingen, het helpen van docenten en studenten en het bijdragen aan vlekkeloze administratieve processen? Kom dan vooral kennismaken!
 Wat ga je doen? 
Jouw focus zal liggen op uitvoerende taken op het gebied van het ondersteunen van de onderwijsprocessen van de bachelor Diergeneeskunde en de minor Basics in Veterinary Medicine (BVM).
Denk aan:
 Mailcontact met bachelor studenten over bijvoorbeeld inschrijvingen, toetsing, inzages;
 Het voorbereiden van tentamens, inzages en verwerken van resultaten;
 Contact met verschillende cursuscoördinatoren van de bachelor Diergeneeskunde die je ondersteunt bij hun cursus door o.a. het invoeren van cijfers, organiseren van toetsing en inzages
 Dagelijks beheer van de mailbox, waarbij je vragen van student, docenten en OWC-collega’s beantwoord;
 Je bent achterwacht van je mede bachelor diergeneeskunde onderwijsondersteuner;
 Eerste aanspreekpunt voor studenten tijdens de inschrijfperiodes en het maken van de onderwijsindeling;
 Aanvragen van surveillanten voor de toetsing en de eerste controle van ingediende facturaties;
 Regelen van de vaccinatie voor eerstejaars studenten Diergeneeskunde;
 Mailcontact met kandidaten die interesse hebben in de minor BVM en het klaarzetten van de studentdossiers voor de selectiecommissie.
In deze functie werk je in onderwijssystemen zoals Osiris, Brightspace en Remindo.
Als onderwijsondersteuner draai je mee in het team Docentondersteuning & Studiepunt (D&S). Het team D&S ondersteunt in het opzetten, uitvoeren en verbeteren van het onderwijs voor de bachelor en masteropleiding Diergeneeskunde en de bacheloropleiding Zorg, gezondheid en samenleving.
Je werkt nauw samen met alle collega's van het Onderwijscentrum, in het bijzonder met de andere onderwijsondersteuners, onderwijscoördinator bachelor Diergeneeskunde, roosteraars, key-users van de onderwijssystemen en de studieadviseurs.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je beschikt over hbo denkniveau;
 maakt je softwareprogramma’s gemakkelijk eigen;
 werkt nauwkeurig en kan goed het overzicht houden bij verschillende werkzaamheden;
 Je werkuren komen overeen met de college-uren van de studenten en docenten;
 hebt een proactieve, flexibele en dienstverlenende instelling ten aanzien van studenten en docenten;
 hebt affiniteit met het (hoger) onderwijsdomein.
 ervaring met onderwijssystemen zoals Osiris, Brightspace is een pre,
 en dat alles met een professionele en prettige communicatiestijl.

academictransfer:364453.description:
Wil jij een team van controlprofessionals begeleiden in een belangrijke nieuwe fase? Bij de Universiteit Utrecht ontstaat één afdeling Control voor de gehele Universitaire Bestuursdienst (UBD). De huidige teams Control van de UBD en Finance & Control van Campus & Facilities komen samen.
Als manager control UBD geef je richting aan die ontwikkeling: je brengt mensen, werkwijzen en expertise bij elkaar, je geeft richting aan het team en de ontwikkeling ervan, en je zorgt voor een professionele, prettige en goed georganiseerde werksituatie en -sfeer.
 Wat ga je doen? 
De samenvoeging van de teams biedt kansen om kennis beter te benutten, werkwijzen te harmoniseren waar dat helpt en de continuïteit te versterken. Tegelijkertijd vraagt dit om een leidinggevende die oog heeft voor mensen, voor verschillen in werkwijze en voor de dagelijkse praktijk.
Je geeft leiding aan een afdeling van ongeveer 15,5 fte. Je bent verantwoordelijk voor de HR-cyclus, teamontwikkeling, werkverdeling en de verdere inrichting van de werkorganisatie. De directeur Financiën, Control & Administratie (FCA) is eindverantwoordelijk voor de regie en coördinatie van de totale UBD-begroting en de P&C-cyclus. Jij zorgt ervoor dat het team goed is toegerust om daaraan bij te dragen.
Je werkzaamheden bestaan onder meer uit:
 leidinggeven aan de controllers en de ondersteunende collega’s;
 medewerkers begeleiden bij de vorming van één afdeling;
 zorgen voor duidelijke rollen, werkafspraken en een passende werkverdeling;
 verbeteren van interne processen en samenwerking;
 het vakmanschap en de onderlinge kennisuitwisseling binnen het team versterken;
 afstemmen met de directeur FCA, de directies binnen de UBD (waaronder met name de directie Campus & Facilities);
 fungeren als toegankelijk aanspreekpunt voor het team en als inhoudelijk gesprekspartner op hoofdlijnen.

REQUIREMENTS:
Je brengt als leidinggevende rust en duidelijkheid, zonder de complexiteit van de omgeving te versimpelen. Je luistert goed, verbindt verschillende perspectieven en neemt besluiten wanneer dat nodig is. Je communiceert helder en zorgvuldig, ook wanneer belangen of opvattingen uiteenlopen. Je creëert een werkomgeving waarin collega’s zich gehoord voelen, elkaar weten te vinden en gezamenlijk verantwoordelijkheid nemen voor het resultaat.
Daarnaast heb je:
 hbo+ of wo werk- en denkniveau;
 ervaring met het leidinggeven aan professionals;
 aantoonbare ervaring met teamontwikkeling, verandering en procesverbetering;
 je hebt affiniteit met financiën, control en bedrijfsvoering. Je begrijpt de hoofdlijnen van het controldomein, stelt de juiste vragen en benut de expertise die in het team aanwezig is
 ervaring met werken in een organisatie met verschillende bestuurlijke en inhoudelijke belangen;
 de vaardigheid om helder en zorgvuldig te communiceren;
 een uitstekende beheersing van het Nederlands (C2) en een werkbare beheersing van het Engels (B1).

academictransfer:364486.description:
Are you fascinated by how eukaryotic genomes evolve? Do you want to look beyond model organisms and point mutations to uncover the complexity of genome evolution? Are you excited to use computational approaches to explore how genome architecture shapes evolution in a collaborative and stimulating environment? We offer a PhD position in our diverse research team in the Laboratory of Microbiology at Wageningen University & Research. As a PhD candidate, you will investigate how 3D chromosome folding influence how the genomes of unicellular eukaryotes evolve. 
 What will you do? 
In this PhD project, you will study the genome evolution and architecture – including 3D folding - of understudied unicellular eukaryotes, using the latest computational approaches.
 As PhD candidate in our lab, you will: 
 utilize cutting-edge bioinformatics tools to trace the evolution of genomes, genes and proteins, including phylogenetic analyses and reconstructions;
 use new genomic data to characterize the large-scale and small-scale genome architectures of understudied eukaryotic microbes;
 deepen our understanding of eukaryotes’ diversification and origins;
 have the opportunity to pursue your own research questions and implement your ideas;
 work independently and develop your own research questions within the project;
 contribute to teaching in our courses and supervising BSc and MSc thesis students;
 work in a stimulating environment that combines evolutionary and cellular research on eukaryotes and prokaryotes, in a team operating at the forefront of unravelling the deep origins of eukaryotic cells, and we are expanding our explorations of the understudied world of eukaryotic microbes.
Even though this is a computational project, it offers possibilities to work on eukaryotic microbes in the wet lab.
 Why? 
Understanding how genomes evolve is fundamental to explaining species diversity and adaptation. Compared with prokaryotes, eukaryotic genomes show a greater structural and functional plasticity, with a larger genome size, a higher proportion of non-coding regions and a more complex 3D chromosome organization. Notably, genome evolution is not only driven by small mutations, but also by larger changes, including chromosome rearrangements and horizontal gene transfer. How and why such changes occur is still an open question, in particular for eukaryotic microbes, which dominate the eukaryotic tree of life but remain poorly studied. Recent studies, conducted in model organisms, suggest that these changes are induced by how chromosomes are folded in 3D. This 3D folding of the chromosomes typically allows distant genomic regions to interact. To gain a deeper understanding of genome evolution, including in non-model organisms, taking 3D chromosome folding into account is a promising direction.
 You will work here 
This PhD position is embedded within the Laboratory of Microbiology , and part of the Genome Evolution and Dynamics in Eukaryotes (GEDE) group, which is led by Dr. Jolien van Hooff . You will be co-supervised by Dr. Ursula Oggenfuss. We aim to get this position filled by March 2027.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an enthusiastic and curiosity-driven candidate with a strong interest in evolution, genomics and microbiology, especially for non-model protists. You have at least some experience with handling biological datasets and a strong drive to learn and apply bioinformatic approaches. Preferably, you have some knowledge of genome-wide analyses and phylogenetics. If you bring experience or a strong interest in the cultivation of protists, we are open to the possibility of integrating cultivation as part of your project.
 You also possess: 
 a MSc degree in Biology, Microbiology, Bioinformatics, or a related field by the start of the project;
 programming experience with languages like Python and R and/or experience in bioinformatics analyses;
 a strong curiosity in learning about biological systems and to work interdisciplinary;
 good organization skills and a high level of independence;
 good verbal and written communication skills, as well as the willingness and ability to work together in teams.
For this position your command of the English language is expected to be at C1 level . Sometimes it is necessary to submit an internationally recognized Certificate of Proficiency in the English Language. More information can be found here .

CONTRACT: 18 months

academictransfer:364522.description:
Institutions for Open Societies (IOS), in collaboration with Utrecht School of Governance, Faculty of Law, Economics & Governance, are honored with the appointment of Prof. Ine Apapoe (Anton de Kom University) as the Prince Claus Chair under the theme: Equity from Within: Maroon Governance and the Future of Development in Suriname. To support this appointment, we are seeking a postdoctoral researcher, based in Utrecht, for the period February 2027 until January 2029. The postdoctoral researcher is co-funded by Utrecht University Fund and IOS.
 Wat ga je doen? 
In this role, you will combine theoretical and methodological expertise in research with organizational and social skills to advance the goals of the Prince Claus Chair (approximately 50-50%). The postdoc position is designed to enhance high-quality academic scholarship and leverage evidence-based research for equity and development, with an emphasis on Suriname and the advancement of Inclusive Governance in Suriname. You will work closely with the Prince Claus chairholder, who will stay in Suriname and Utrecht on an alternating basis.
The postdoctoral researcher will play a pivotal role in advancing the strategic and academic objectives of the Prince Claus Chair. The research focuses on the intersection of traditional governance, post-slavery legacies, and inclusive development. By placing Maroon communities of Suriname in a comparative global context, alongside other traditional societies and African-descended populations, the PCC aims to deepen our understanding of shared governance challenges and strategies. The research by the PCC fits into current societal debates around ethnicity, land rights, sustainable development, female leadership and the social, economic and environmental impacts of utilizing natural resources. It provides an opportunity to explore important themes such as equity, access to resources, and the participation of marginalized populations. This will come together under the heading of Inclusive Governance. Research will have academic and societal impact, and tasks include:
 Designing and conducting research approaches and methodologies aimed at understanding Inclusive Governance, in Suriname and elsewhere;
 Involvement in developing research proposals, instrumentation, design, sampling, data collection, data analysis and report writing;
 Publishing and disseminating research findings to (inter)national academic, public and practical audiences, in multiple (written and oral) ways;
 Editorial roles related to writing and publishing a book on public governance and administration in Suriname.
Approximately half of the available time is reserved for research aligned with the PCC’s goals, and half of the time for organizational and project support. The postdoctoral researcher will be the first point of contact for these organizational activities and will work closely with the chair holder, prof. Ine Apapoe, and representatives of the strategic theme Institutions for Open Societies, Utrecht School of Governance, REBO faculty, and other key stakeholders to ensure the successful implementation of the chair’s objectives.
Key organizational responsibilities will include:
 Facilitating international collaboration, for example by organizing events;
 Developing and implementing activities within UU (IOS and REBO/USG) in line with the workplan of the PCC;
 Managing the visits of the PCC to Utrecht, and organizing events, workshops and meetings;
 Practical support to the PCC related to proposal writing, book publishing and other activities as defined in the workplan.

REQUIREMENTS:
The researcher will be expected to demonstrate strong social and organizational skills, extensive methodological expertise, cultural awareness, the ability to work collaboratively in an international academic environment, and a commitment to advancing (interdisciplinary) research and practice. Knowledge on and/or previous working experience in Suriname is an asset.
 Successful candidates have the following qualifications: Ongoing or completed PhD in a relevant field;
 Written and oral fluency in Dutch and English;
 Expertise in both qualitative and quantitative research methodologies;
 A keen interest in governance, institutions, equity and development, and global perspectives;
 Experience with interdisciplinary research projects;
 Strong social and organizational skills, including project management and event coordination;
 A proactive attitude, with a hands-on but (culturally) sensitive work approach;

academictransfer:364539.description:
Are you passionate about uncovering what happens to the brain during impact, subsequent injury, and how the injury evolves, or resolves, afterwards? Then we are inviting you to join our interdisciplinary research teams to explore the long-term effects of repetitive mild traumatic brain injury (rmTBI) in both humans and mouse models.
 Join Us! 
Repeated mild head injuries, the kind sustained by athletes, veterans, and victims of domestic violence, are increasingly linked to long-term memory problems and a higher risk of dementia later in life. Strikingly, these lasting effects can occur even when no visible brain damage is present. Yet, we still don't understand how repeated injury leads to memory decline, and there are currently no treatments that address these long-term cognitive consequences.
In this project, our main goal is to dissect the molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying traumatic brain injury (TBI)-associated neurodegeneration, memory decline and dementia. To this end we will study how rmTBI triggers long-term neuroinflammation and cellular and functional changes in the hippocampus, leading to memory impairment, as TBI may weaken the brain’s resilience, accelerating hippocampus-related memory decline.
This project is a collaboration between the Fitzsimons lab at the Swammerdam Institute for Life Sciences, University of Amsterdam (UvA) and the Salta lab at the Netherlands Institute for Neuroscience (NIN). The complementary expertise and conceptual understanding of brain insult models, specifically TBI mouse models, transcriptomics, detailed analysis of cell morphology, advanced microscopy techniques, neurodegenerative diseases, adult neurogenesis analysis in human brain samples and human iPSC culture and derivation, establish an ideal conceptual and technical synergy, making our team very well-suited to identify disease mechanisms involved in neurodegenerative diseases associated with repetitive TBI.
This project is funded by the Nederlandse Organisatie voor Wetenschappelijk Onderzoek (NWO), Open Competition Domain Science – M2 grant and will be performed at the Swammerdam Institute for Life Sciences, University of Amsterdam. It is a follow up of our previous published work on traumatic brain injury and adult hippocampal neurogenesis:
 https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-49299-6 
 https://www.nature.com/articles/s41590-022-01208-z 
 https://www.cell.com/cell-stem-cell/fulltext/S1934-5909(26)00142-6?_returnURL=https%3A%2F%2Flinkinghub.elsevier.com%2Fretrieve%2Fpii%2FS1934590926001426%3Fshowall%3Dtrue 
 https://www.cell.com/cell-stem-cell/fulltext/S1934-5909(26)00037-8?_returnURL=https%3A%2F%2Flinkinghub.elsevier.com%2Fretrieve%2Fpii%2FS1934590926000378%3Fshowall%3Dtrue 
We welcome applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds, experiences and perspectives.
 This is what you will do 
As a PhD candidate in our group, you will characterize the short- and long-term effects of rmTBI on the fate of resident neural stem cells in the hippocampus, including neurogenesis, astrogliogenesis, reactive astrogliosis, production of inflammatory mediators and, microglia activation, hippocampal function and memory impairment in a mouse model of repetitive TBI. Moreover, you will contribute to the identification of conserved cell populations, transcriptional programs and intercellular communication networks affected by TBI in the mouse and human hippocampus.
For this, you will use an established mouse model of closed head rmTBI in combination with single-nucleus/spatial transcriptomics, cellular morphology, electrophysiology, behavioural testing, and molecular assays to identify mechanisms linking rmTBI to persistent hippocampal dysfunction and neuroinflammation. All the necessary techniques, expertise and training opportunities are available in our labs.
Tasks and responsibilities:
 complete a PhD thesis within the official appointment duration (four years);
 systematically and independently designing, performing and documenting experiments;
 setting up and running bioinformatic pipelines to complement experimental work;
 be an active and responsible member of both our research groups and discuss work with group members during departmental meetings and incorporate their feedback;
 taking a leading role in writing manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals;
 participating in PhD training programme of the University of Amsterdam and The Graduate School Neurosciences Amsterdam Rotterdam (ONWAR);
 assist teaching and supervision of Bachelor and Master students at the UvA.
You will get the opportunity to:
 present results at (inter)national scientific conferences;
 expand your academic, professional and personal skills;
 contribute to science communication and outreach activities;
 thrive in a team that values impactful research and mutual support;
 combine the advantages that a collaborative project offers, such as access to state-of-the art research facilities at both the Swammerdam Inst

academictransfer:364546.description:
Are you an experienced linguistic typologist with a keen interest in interdisciplinary approaches to cross-linguistic research? Do you imagine yourself working in a small team at the crossroads of linguistics and cognitive science? Then we welcome your application for a postdoctoral position in our multi-methods project on negation across languages.
 Come work with us 
In this project you collaborate in a small international team of (psycho)linguists and cognitive scientists on the evolution and processing of negation in language. Together, we investigate the cognitive underpinnings of why most languages prefer to express the negator early rather than late in the utterance. Alongside one of the PIs and another part-time postdoctoral researcher, you will carry out a typological study on languages with and without late negation, with specific attention to languages of Papua, South America, and sub-Saharan Africa. In addition, you will contribute to a multi-methods project including artificial language learning and EEG, as well field-based experiments with speakers of Cha’palaa, an indigenous language of Ecuador.
 This is what you will be doing 
 Design and conduct qualitative and quantitative typological research on the typology of (early/late) negation and its structural correlates, in collaboration with one of the PIs and another part-time (20%) postdoctoral researcher;
 Collaborate with the rest of the team (another PI, PhD candidate, and external partner) in an interdisciplinary project, including (field-based) psycholinguistic experiments;
 Co-authoring at least three papers and compiling a typological database;
 Co-organizing and contributing to a summer school and a workshop.
 This is what we ask of you 
 PhD in a relevant field: (morpho-syntactic/semantic) typology, cross-linguistic psycholinguistics;
 Experience with structure of non-WEIRD languages, in particular languages of South-America, Papua, and/or sub-Saharan Africa;
 Experience with qualitative and quantitative approaches to cross-linguistic variation;
 Demonstrable affinity with interdisciplinary and (field-based) experimental approaches;
 A cooperative attitude and excellent communicative skills;
 Good command of (academic) English;
 Optional: working knowledge of Spanish.
 This is what else we offer you 
 The employment will be for one year. Contingent on a positive performance evaluation the contract will be extended with two years. The preferred starting date is 01 March 2027;
 Depending on relevant experience, the salary at the start of the employment is between €3.706 and €5.760 euros gross per month based on full-time employment for a 38-hour working week. In addition, you will receive an 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% end-of-year allowance. The Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities is applicable.
 This is where you will be working 
This postdoctoral position is part of an NWO-funded Open Competition Large project, led by two PIs: Eva van Lier and Marieke Schouwstra, affiliated with two research schools: the Amsterdam Center for Language and Communication (ACLC) and the Institute for Logic, Language and Computation (ILLC). In addition, our research team includes another (part-time) postdoc, a PhD candidate, and an in international specialist in psycholinguistics.
For questions about the position or department, you can contact us during office hours at:
Eva van Lier ( e.h.vanlier@uva.nl ) and/or Marieke Schouwstra (m.schouwstra@uva.nl)
 Application 
If you recognize yourself in the profile and are interested in the position, we look forward to receiving your application.
The vacancy closes on 9 November 2026. The first round of interviews will take place on 7 December 2026

