Join us!
 We are looking for two ambitious PhD Researchers for the four-year project “Agents of doubt: A novel framework for understanding the impact of synthetic content on adolescents’ knowledge formation.” The PhD Candidates are embedded in the Amsterdam School of Communication Research (ASCoR) and are part of the Youth and Media Entertainment Program Group . You will be working together with Jochen Peter and Toni van der Meer. 
 What are you going to do
 Adolescents heavily use synthetic content – AI-generated text, audio, images, and video. However, given their still developing cognitive skills, they may also be least equipped to deal with synthetic content. This project investigates how synthetic content reshapes how adolescents develop knowledge. We propose a novel framework that links synthetic content to adolescents’ increased doubt about what can be reliably known (epistemic uncertainty) and their diminishing capacity to form beliefs independently (epistemic agency). Epistemic uncertainty and reduced epistemic agency, in turn, may lead to a collapse of shared norms of knowledge, distrust in epistemic authorities, and declining intellectual engagement (epistemic anomie). 
 This project uses several quantitative and qualitative methods like (panel) survey, lab experiments focus groups, and data donation. Each PhD Candidate will write a paper-based dissertation and strive to publish the results of the individual sub-studies in journals in the field of communication science and related disciplines.
 This project consists of two PhD projects and is funded by the Dutch Research Foundation (NWO) through an Open Competition L grant. Therefore, successful candidates are also expected to participate in activities related to the overall project. The PhD project will be supervised by prof. dr. Jochen Peter and dr. Toni van der Meer. The preferred starting date for the PhD Candidate is 1 January 2027.
 Tasks include:
 completing an English-language PhD dissertation consisting of research articles within the appointment period;
 conducting (panel) surveys, lab experiments, data donation, and qualitative focus groups;
 conducting data analysis;
 presenting the work at national and international conferences;
 spending 10% of your time on teaching-related tasks.
 What do you have to offer
 You have:
 a (research) master’s degree in Communication Science or in another social science discipline relevant to the project, such as Political Science, Sociology, or (Social) Psychology;
 experience with aspects related to the topic of the proposal (e.g., digital communication, artificial intelligence, developmental research, research on mis/disinformation);
 experience with quantitative research methods (e.g., (panel) survey, (lab) experiment, data donation), ideally already demonstrated in your thesis or other research projects you were involved in;
 experience with various techniques of quantitative statistical data analysis, experience with longitudinal analyses is a plus;
 experience with, or knowledge of, focus groups and analysis of qualitative data;
 an excellent spoken and written command of English (as you will write an English-language dissertation and present at international conferences);
 an excellent spoken and written command of Dutch is a plus;
 the motivation and ability to work in an interdisciplinary team.
 You are/have:
 passionate about research, particularly related to adolescents, misinformation, and/or artificial intelligence;
 an affinity with the topic of the project;
 open to feedback;
 committed to open science;
 able to work independently and in a team;
 eager to learn and to develop new (academic) skills;
 organized, flexible, and have attention to detail.
 What else do we offer you
 The position concerns temporary employment of 1.0 fte (38 hours per week) for a maximum term of 4 years. The initial employment is for one year. Following a positive assessment and barring altered financial and organizational circumstances, this term will be extended by a maximum of 3 years, which should result in the conferral of a doctorate. We will put together a curriculum which will also include the opportunity to attend training courses and both national and international events. You will also be tasked with teaching Bachelor's students.
 For this position the University Job Classification profile “PhD Candidate” applies. 
 Your salary will be [€3,204] gross per month in the first year and will increase to [€4,051] in the final year, based on full-time employment and in keeping with the Collective Labour Agreement of Dutch Universities . We additionally offer an extensive package of secondary benefits, including 8% holiday allowance and a year-end bonus of 8.3%.
 The UvA offers excellent possibilities for further professional development and education.
 In this department, you will work
 The Amsterdam School of Communication Research  (ASCoR) is the research institute for the Communication Science Department in the Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences at the University of Amsterdam.
 A challenging work environment with a variety of duties and ample scope for individual initiative and development within an inspiring organization. The social and behavioral sciences play a leading role in addressing the major societal challenges faced by the world, the Netherlands and Amsterdam, now and in the future.
