We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher to work on the governance of transitions towards more sustainable and regenerative farming systems. Governance instruments — including certification schemes, private standards, and policy incentives — play an important role in shaping the future of livestock farming systems. Are you curious about how standards, incentives, and sustainability indicators influence farming systems and farmers’ livelihoods? Are you excited to contribute to interdisciplinary research that supports transitions towards more sustainable farming systems? Then we would love to hear from you. 
We are looking for a candidate with experience in the governance of farming systems, who is highly motivated to contribute to more sustainable food systems through inter- and transdisciplinary research. You are a systems thinker with an interest in public-private governance of livestock farming systems, agricultural policy and/or sustainable finance. You are familiar with current policy debates on the future of European and Dutch agriculture and enjoy working at the interface of science, policy and practice.
You will strengthen the team working on farming systems governance by contributing to research on public and private governance instruments, sustainability indicators, and perceptions of those affected by transitions. Your work will include contributing to ongoing research on regenerative agriculture (including ReGeNL), facilitating stakeholder engagement, supporting project management, developing research proposals, and contributing to education. You are encouraged to describe relevant experience with these tasks in your CV and motivation letter.
 You will work here 
This postdoc will be situated at the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. Here we analyze and explore the roles of livestock in food systems with the aim of making these food systems more sustainable. The group focuses on unravelling the complexity of livestock systems, and exploring trade-offs and synergies of innovations regarding environmental impact, animal welfare, and economic viability, to design a more sustainable future.
You will join the team led by Dr. Evelien de Olde and contribute to research on governance issues in farming systems within a polycentric food systems context, including sustainability certification and standards, the role of metrics and KPIs, and the impact of sustainability transitions on livestock farming systems.

REQUIREMENTS:
A completed PhD degree with an interdisciplinary focus in a relevant field such as sustainability science, agricultural science, animal science, political science, environmental science, or related disciplines;
 Curiosity to explore leverage points and complexities in the governance of farming systems while working in transdisciplinary settings with project partners including farmers;
 Affinity with farming systems research, sustainability indicators, and governance theories ;
 Experience with diverse qualitative and/or mixed-method research methods such as interviews, focus groups, serious games, and stakeholder engagement;
 Interest in developing research proposals and contributing to future projects;
 You are a good team player, collaborative and creative who can communicate easily with diverse audiences and act proactively;
 You posses strong communication skills, both verbal and written, in Dutch and English (C1 level);
 You are able and willing to work from Wageningen at least 3 days a week.

CONTRACT: 2 years

euraxess:469911.description:
As a Marine Ecologist specialized in the Delta ecosystems at Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) in Yerseke, you will play an active role in restoring and enhancing the unique natural environment of the Delta waters. Using innovative, sustainable solutions based on the philosophy of ‘building with nature’, you will translate scientific knowledge into practical applications with a tangible impact.
The South-Western Delta presents major ecological challenges as well as opportunities. From the sand deficiency in the Oosterschelde to nature conservation in the Westerschelde and improving water quality in Delta waters such as Lake Grevelingen and Lake Veere: you will make a significant contribution to solutions that make a difference to nature and society.
Together with other experts in the field of marine ecology, you will work on a wide range of delta-related issues. You will investigate where nature development and nature-based solutions can be applied to areas such as flood protection and water quality improvement and will be able to convincingly demonstrate to clients the added value of this sustainable approach. In addition, you will contribute to the scientific basis of the Nature Restoration Plan and the management plans for Natura 2000 sites in the South-Western Delta. Liaising with clients and stakeholders is an integral part of your work.
 Your duties and responsibilities as a Marine Ecologist include: 
 contribute to a wide range of literature reviews, data analyses, and field and laboratory experiments in the field of nature conservation and benthic fauna research;
 assist with securing new projects and drafting research proposals;
 communicate with fellow researchers and clients;
 report and present research findings to clients, attend conferences and take part in workshops;
 publish and present results in academic journals and at conferences.
 Your team 
Based at WMR’s site in Yerseke, you will work alongside various project teams of differing compositions. As a project leader, you will provide substantive and project-related guidance to fellow researchers so that they can carry out their work efficiently and thoroughly. As a project member, you will often focus on a specific area that matches your expertise.
 You will work here 
 Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is one of the nine institutes that form part of Wageningen Research. WMR is a leading, independent research institute with sites in Yerseke, IJmuiden and Den Helder, which focuses on strategic and applied marine ecological research. Products and services are delivered on a project basis and are diverse in nature: field research, realistic-scale experiments, exploratory laboratory-scale studies, data management and modelling. The institute has modern research facilities and is ISO 9001 certified.
Your area of work will primarily be the south-western Delta, but other parts of the Netherlands, such as the North Sea and the Wadden Sea, as well as international projects, are also possibilities.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are curious about the interactions within ecosystems and are committed to contributing to the protection and restoration of marine and coastal ecosystems. Thanks to your ability to take a broader perspective, you feel at home at the interface between science and policy. You naturally build professional relationships with others and value each team member’s contribution to the overall project outcome. You can cope with time pressure and plan your work to meet deadlines. You are driven, proactive, collaborative, customer-focused and a strong communicator.
 You also possess: 
 a successfully completed relevant academic degree in the natural sciences or life sciences. A PhD in the field specified for this post is an advantage;
 experience with nature based solutions for flood protection;
 demonstrable work experience in ecological estuary and Delta research;
 a good command of both Dutch and English.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level B2.

CONTRACT: 1 year

euraxess:471910.description:
The challenge:
In the past decades, photographs and videos of events were regarded as reliable evidence for both news media and forensic investigations. However, recent developments in AI technology now make it possible, with minimal technological knowledge or skills, to create extremely convincing manipulated or synthesised images, audio and video data, often called DeepFakes.
This fact is a serious threat to the trustworthiness of news in the media and forensic processes in criminal investigations. Therefore, development of reliable methods to detect manipulations and distinguish bonafide from synthesised data is of utmost importance. In addition, especially for forensic investigations, simple detection is not sufficient. An explanation of the traces of manipulation and tools used for the synthesis or
manipulation should be produced as well.
In the EU project DEFORM, 14 research institutes, universities and companies from 6 different countries aim to develop methods and tools for explainable detection of DeepFakes using Foundation models and other techniques.
At the University of Twente, we are looking for a PhD
researcher who will work in close collaboration with the
other partners on detection of fake videos using
inconsistencies in video as well as audio.

ADDITIONAL INFORMATION:
Benefits A fully funded, full-time PhD position for four years, with a qualifier in the first year and an intended start in autumn 2026.
 A dynamic research environment with enthusiastic colleagues and close interaction between measurement science and power electronics. A working culture that encourages independence, responsibility and close collaboration. A working culture that encourages independence, responsibility and close collaboration.
 Your salary and associated conditions are in accordance with the collective labour agreement for Dutch universities (CAO-NU);
 You will receive a gross monthly salary ranging from € 3.204,- (first year) to € 4.051,- (fourth year);
 There are excellent benefits including a holiday allowance of 8% of the gross annual salary, an end-of-year bonus of 8.3%, and a solid pension scheme;
