uva:15501.description:
€3,706 - €5,760; Master's; Minimum 15.2 hours
Shape a living lab for data, AI and democracy 
 The DATAGOV Living Lab is not a conventional research laboratory: it is a space where researchers, artists, public institutions, civil society organisations and citizens experiment together with participatory research, play and game-based methods, and arts-based engagement. Activities range from the world-building game Le Grand Jeu to participatory AI audits, infrastructure walks and an art programme. As Lab Manager, you turn this ambition into a lively, sustainable programme of workshops and events, working closely with the project leader Prof. Stefania Milan and the DATAGOV research team while helping shape the Lab's own methods and formats.
 This is what you will be doing
 You coordinate the Lab's programme of workshops, experiments and data collection, as well as public events, in collaboration with the research team;
 You help design and facilitate participatory, play-based and game-based research activities;
 You support arts-based and creative forms of research and public engagement, working together with artists and creative practitioners;
 You translate research questions into engaging experimental formats, helping researchers develop activities for the Lab;
 You coordinate collaborations with academic and non-academic partners, including public institutions, civil society organisations, artists, designers and technology practitioners;
 You support experiments involving digital technologies, coding and AI applications, liaising between researchers and technical collaborators;
 You manage the practical organisation of the Lab: planning, scheduling, documentation, budgets and materials;
 You document activities and contribute to project reporting, communication and dissemination.
 This is what we ask of you
 You are organised, creative and proactive, and just as comfortable taking the lead as working as part of a team. Specifically:
 You have hands-on experience with participatory, play-based, game-based or other experimental research or engagement methods, and with the design of research-oriented games such as Le Grand Jeu ;
