wur:16656.description:
The Laboratory of Phytopathology offers two new positions for Associate Professors with ambition to grow who will help shape the future of phytopathology through innovative research on disease resilience, pathogen adaptation, quantitative disease biology, predictive modelling, or mechanistic understanding of plant-pathogen interactions.
As an Associate Professor in the Laboratory of Phytopathology, you will contribute to working on the frontier of phytopathology. You enjoy and have experience in developing novel concepts in plant-pathogen interactions on different scales (individual plant, population, ecosystem). You are a team player and able to connect to researchers in other chair groups at Wageningen University and establish an (inter)national network. You contribute to teaching and to mentoring colleagues in the team. You have the ambition to contribute to positioning the group in a competitive (funding) landscape.
Specific topics that are complementary to the current research group are:
 theoretical phytopathology;
 predictive modelling / Bioinformatics;
 quantitative disease immunity;
 structural biology.
However, researchers working on other cutting-edge theories/approaches in phytopathology are also encouraged to apply.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 develop novel concepts in plant-pathogen interactions;
 contribute to the funding of the group;
 contribute to (coordination and innovation of) teaching and supervision of PhD students and postdocs;
 develop (inter)national network and collaborations;
 mentoring and coaching of scientific staff.
 Your team 
You will work in a highly interdisciplinary and international research environment within the Laboratory of Phytopathology at Wageningen University. Currently, the permanent staff of the group consists of 5 researchers, 2 teachers, and 6 support staff.
 You will work here 
 Laboratory of Phytopathology at Wageningen University.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an experienced and innovative researcher in phytopathology at the Associate Professor level and have the ambition to further develop your own position as well as the position of the Laboratory of Phytopathology. You are dedicated to teaching students. You are experienced in navigating the (inter)national funding landscape.
 You also possess: 
 vision on how to operate at the frontier of international phytopathology research;
 strong communication/mentoring skills, potential to act as an ambassador of the group;
 track record in initiating/participating in large consortia and Public-Private Partnerships;
 proven networking skills;
 feeling for translating research and connecting with industries and society, e.g. by working on (model) crops.
In our international working environment, there is an increasing amount of English-language communication. For this position, it is essential to have excellent English language skills.

CONTRACT: 1 year

wur:16693.description:
Are you a motivated and proactive colleague who would like to join our Shared Research Facilities (SRF) team in Wageningen and contribute to further developing our research facilities strategy, with the aim of creating greater impact and value through our advanced equipment and research facilities?
Advanced research facilities are an essential part of Wageningen University & Research’s (WUR) innovation and value creation strategy, enabling us to develop solutions that have an impact on the challenges our world faces. Innovative, state-of-the-art research equipment and facilities are essential in order to conduct world-class research into healthy food and a sustainable living environment. Our team is positioned within the Value Creation department and is responsible for coordinating investments in research infrastructure, optimising collaboration and creating value. To achieve this, we make research facilities more visible and encourage the sharing of research facilities both within our organisation and with external parties, many of which are also located on Wageningen Campus. In doing so, the colleagues in the SRF team work closely with colleagues from the Wageningen Campus team.
We are looking for an enterprising Programme Manager who, as part of the SRF team, will work both independently and as part of a team to develop strategic WUR policies for the further development of (shared) research facilities and to realise substantial investments in advanced research equipment. In addition, the Programme Manager will act as Secretary to the SRF Scientific Advisory Board and as Secretary to the SRF Steering Committee.
Being part of the SRF team means being at the heart of innovation processes involving a wide range of people and organisations. In addition to colleagues from Wageningen Campus, internal stakeholders include professors, researchers and management teams from WUR’s five Science Groups, as well as colleagues from Finance & Control, IT and Procurement. There is also extensive interaction with external organisations, including other universities, businesses (including start-ups and other SMEs), public sector organisations such as the Province of Gelderland and the Ministries of Economic Affairs and Climate Policy and Agriculture, Fisheries, Food Security and Nature, as well as organisations such as Foodvalley and Oost NL.
 The role of Programme Manager SRF involves: 
 secretary role: Acting as General Secretary to the SRF Scientific Advisory Board (SAB) and General Secretary to the SRF Steering Committee, providing administrative, communicational and organisational support within the SAB and Steering Committee. Initiating and coordinating both internal and external advisory processes and strategic decision-making regarding the funding of research facilities;
 coordinating and shaping the process from initial idea to the realisation of research facilities within WUR, involving and connecting all relevant stakeholders and playing an active role in strategically developing and deploying the SRF Roadmap;
 further developing our network of (shared) research facilities with both internal and external partners;
 acting as the point of contact for the sharing of research facilities for both internal and external stakeholders;
 grant implementation: Initiating and coordinating investments in shared research equipment and facilities, communicating and reporting on progress to funding bodies, and monitoring the impact of shared research equipment;
 exploring additional funding opportunities and independently preparing and submitting new grant applications. Experience with grant application processes is a plus.
 co-developing and organising meetings and communication activities relating to SRF;
 working together with SRF and Wageningen Campus colleagues to develop a long-term strategy for WUR and the Wageningen Campus ecosystem, with the aim of generating maximum impact and value from research facilities.

REQUIREMENTS:
For this position, we are looking for a highly motivated and enthusiastic Programme Manager who: 
 is a team player who operates at an academic level and is familiar with the WUR domain;
 has at least five years of recent relevant professional experience and is well acquainted with R&D and the facilities required to support it;
 has excellent written and verbal communication skills, in Dutch (minimum C1 level, hence fluent in Dutch is mandatory) and English;
 can represent WUR internally and externally at various levels;
 has excellent relationship management skills;
 is proactive and flexible;
 combines strong organisational skills with interpersonal sensitivity.

wur:16699.description:
Grasslands cover large areas in Europe and foster potential to provide multiple functions, such as ecosystem services. The Horizon Europe GrassConnect project aims to study the effects of farm management on ecosystem service provision, biodiversity, grassland productivity, cultural value, and people’s livelihoods.
GrassConnect is a recently granted, interdisciplinary, and multi-actor European project with 31 partners in 14 countries. GrassConnect will address challenges derived from grassland intensification, by assessing multifunctionality strategies at field, farm and supply-chain level, and aid decision-making to increase the provision of ecosystem services from grasslands.
Within this project, we offer a PhD position ‘Exploring grassland multifunctionality through modelling ’ to study the effects of management in grass-based farms in the Netherlands on biodiversity. This PhD position will focus on grass-based production systems in the Netherlands, whereas GrassConnect will offer the opportunity to engage with other partners covering different grassland systems, production systems and pedo-climatic conditions. Some farm data is available from past and ongoing projects.
After searching, selecting, and analyzing, the data will be used to assess the effects of management on biodiversity and ecosystem services applying modelling approaches from the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. The candidate will be based on the campus of Wageningen University & Research.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 Develop a Research Proposal and a Training and Supervision Plan, to structure the 4-year PhD trajectory;
 Master the state of the art in ecosystem services delivery from grasslands under diverse management regimes;
 Investigate mechanistic relationships between grassland management, ecosystem services and livestock productivity (e.g. regression analysis, mechanistic modelling, meta-analysis);
 Assess environmental and socioeconomic performance of dairy farms under diverse scenarios using a bio-economic optimization model (Dairy LP);
 Disseminate the research results by writing papers and presenting your work at international conferences.
 You will work here 
This PhD will be situated at the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. Here we analyze and explore the roles of livestock in food systems with the aim of making these food systems more sustainable. The group focuses on unravelling the complexity of livestock systems and exploring trade-offs and synergies of innovations regarding environmental impact, animal welfare, and economic viability, to design a more sustainable future.
This PhD project is at the interface of biodiversity and modelling domains within the Animal Production Systems group. You will work under the supervision of Dr. Raimon Ripoll-Bosch and Dr. Aart van der Linden . Raimon Ripoll-Bosch focuses on understanding the complex interactions between livestock, nature (biodiversity and ecosystem services) and economy, to design enabling environments for the transitions to nature-inclusive agroecosystems. Aart van der Linden focuses on modelling farm systems with crops and livestock, including ruminant production on grasslands to improve resource use efficiency and reduce environmental impacts.

REQUIREMENTS:
You have strong quantitative and analytical skills and excel in collaborating in large and multi-disciplinary teams. You are a good communicator to both fellow scientists and farmers, open to learn about different types of models, and passionate about grass-based agriculture.
 You also have: 
 Demonstrated acquaintance with dynamic, mechanistic models and/or optimization models;
 Experience with programming in R, GAMS or Python;
 A successfully completed MSc degree in a relevant field, such as Agronomy, Animal Sciences, Plant Science, Environmental Sciences or Integrative Agricultural Sciences
 Affinity with the systems approach and/or interdisciplinary research;
 Excellent writing skills.
For this position, the excellent command of English language (level C1) is a prerequisite. In some cases, internationally recognised certificate may be required.

CONTRACT: 18 months

wur:16700.description:
As an experienced Senior Marine Ecologist at Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) in Yerseke, you will lead a team of dedicated researchers who are working together on a range of ecologically relevant issues of societal importance in the South-Western Delta.
As a Senior Marine Ecologist, specialized in benthic organisms and shellfish, you will use your knowledge and experience to carry out research in the South-Western Delta. You will lead a team of enthusiastic fellow ecologists with whom you will carry out research focusing on the effects of human activities such as fishing and sand replenishment, and the consequences of climate change on marine benthic fauna. You will contribute to nature conservation through the active restoration of shellfish beds in the North Sea and the Delta. You have an affinity for applied research and working in complex policy and stakeholder environments. You will initiate new research, including securing funding for it, and lead major projects.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 to act as a leading figure in the field of benthic ecology in contacts with (potential) clients and the media;
 to manage and carry out large-scale and complex research projects;
 securing new research and drafting multidisciplinary research proposals;
 maintaining and expanding the national and international network in the field of delta- and benthic ecology and the restoration of shellfish beds;
 presenting the research findings to the business community, sector organizations, civil society organizations, government bodies and fellow scientists.
 Your team 
Based at WMR’s site in Yerseke, you will work alongside various project teams of differing compositions. As a project leader, you will provide substantive and project-related guidance to field staff, junior and mid-level researchers, enabling them to carry out their work efficiently and thoroughly.
 You will work here 
 Wageningen Marine Research (WMR) is one of the nine institutes that form part of Wageningen Research. WMR is a leading, independent research institute with sites in Yerseke, IJmuiden and Den Helder, which focuses on strategic and applied marine ecological research. Products and services are delivered on a project basis and are diverse in nature: field research, realistic-scale experiments, exploratory laboratory-scale studies, data management and modelling. The institute has modern research facilities and is ISO 9001 certified.
Your area of work will primarily be the south-western Delta, but other parts of the Netherlands, such as the North Sea and the Wadden Sea, as well as international projects, are also possibilities.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are proactive, results-oriented and have excellent communication and organizational skills. You are able to maintain an overview of complex processes, work with precision and feel at ease in an environment where different interests and disciplines come together.
 You also possess: 
 relevant university degree and PhD in Marine Ecology;
 extensive work experience as a researcher and project leader in the field of benthic ecology;
 entrepreneurial spirit, decisiveness, the ability to take the initiative, good teamwork skills, and a customer- and results-oriented approach;
 a good command of the Dutch language is a strong advantage.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level B2. If you haven’t yet reached this level, we will of course help you, and you are welcome to visit our language centre, Wageningen in’to Language .

CONTRACT: 1 year

wur:16705.description:
Are you motivated to advance operational forest disturbance monitoring using radar satellite data and to contribute to global efforts to understand and combat forest loss? Then this positions might be your perfect next step! 
We are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to advance near-real-time forest disturbance and regrowth monitoring using radar satellite observations. The position combines methodological research with the further development and operation of the RADD (Radar for Detecting Deforestation) forest disturbance alerts , using established and newly available radar satellite missions, including Sentinel-1 and NISAR.
Radar remote sensing is particularly valuable for forest monitoring because of its sensitivity to vegetation structure and its ability to provide systematic observations largely independent of daylight and cloud cover. Translating these observations into accurate, consistent, and operational information on forest disturbance and post-disturbance regrowth, however, remains challenging. Radar responses are influenced by wavelength, acquisition geometry, forest structure, seasonality, and environmental conditions.
The project will address these challenges in two main directions. First, you will extend the RADD framework from disturbance detection towards continuous forest change monitoring, including the detection and characterization of post-disturbance forest regrowth and structural recovery. Second, you will expand the geographic applicability of the RADD alerts beyond their current implementations in the humid tropics and Europe.
The position is embedded in a strong international and interdisciplinary research environment, including collaboration with partners from Global Nature Watch. The project provides opportunities to contribute to high-impact scientific publications, international collaborations, and operational monitoring products with direct societal relevance.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 operational lead of the RADD near-real-time alert system;
 developing and implementing methods for radar-based methods for detecting and monitoring post-disturbance forest regrowth and structural recovery;
 studying forest disturbance-recovery dynamics;
 coordinating project deliverables and ensuring their timely and high-quality completion;
 contributing to collaborative research in an international consortium;
 publishing research results in leading peer-reviewed journals;
 communicating research findings and monitoring developments to scientific, policy, and stakeholder audiences.
 Your team 
You will join the radar remote sensing team within the Laboratory of Geo-information Science and Remote Sensing . The team studies human activities and the dynamics of forest ecosystems from regional to global scales using radar remote sensing. Its work focuses in particular on fundamental radar and multi-sensor methods, near-real-time change monitoring, and the integration of satellite observations with in-situ field data.
 You will work here 
You will work as part of a research team within the Laboratory of Geo-Information Science and Remote Sensing (GRS), which is led by Dr. Johannes Reiche .

REQUIREMENTS:
You are a highly motivated and independent researcher with strong methodological skills and demonstrated expertise in (radar) remote sensing. You have a clear interest in multidisciplinary research at the interface of remote sensing, forest ecology, and forest management.
You combine scientific rigor with the ability to work collaboratively in an international research environment and to translate methodological advances into robust monitoring approaches.
 You also possess: 
 a PhD in remote sensing, data science, forest ecology or a related field;
 a strong foundation in remote sensing, including demonstrated experience with radar/SAR remote sensing;
 experience with forest change, disturbance, regrowth, or recovery dynamics;
 experience developing reproducible data-processing and analysis workflows;
 excellent programming skills, with strong proficiency in Python;
 the ability to publish scientific research and communicate results effectively;
 experience working in interdisciplinary teams and with stakeholders;
 excellent written and spoken English skills (C1 level).
 Preferred Qualifications: 
The following qualifications are considered an advantage:
 experience with satellite time-series analysis, change detection and near-real-time forest monitoring;
 experience with Sentinel-1, NISAR, and/or other spaceborne radar missions;
 experience with scalable Earth observation processing using cloud-computing or high-performance computing environments;
 knowledge of forest disturbance ecology, forest management, and post-disturbance vegetation dynamics;
 experience engaging with operational users, policy actors, or other stakeholders.

CONTRACT: 2 years

wur:16706.description:
Medior Software Developer AI
Your job
 Are you a software developer who likes to build, is curious about AI and wants to contribute to a safe and responsible way in which our university uses this technology? Help us build the way our university uses AI 
 Artificial Intelligence is changing the way we learn, do research and work. As a university, we want to give our students, lecturers and researchers the space to discover and use these opportunities. At the same time, we think it is important that this is done in a safe, responsible and transparent manner. 
 That is why we are building our own GenAI platform, including the WUR AI Chat and the WUR LLM Hub: a secure environment in which users can access modern AI capabilities, within clear frameworks and with attention to privacy, security, academic integrity and responsible use.
 For the team that develops this environment, we are looking for a Medior Software Developer who enjoys building software at the intersection of technology, education, research and Artificial Intelligence. The multidisciplinary team consists of software developers, AI specialists and other colleagues. We work closely with students, lecturers, researchers and colleagues within the university.
 We believe in technical craftsmanship, open collaboration and room to experiment. Not every question has a clear answer in advance. We therefore expect you not only to implement what is on the backlog, but also to ask questions, investigate alternatives and come up with ideas.
 As a Medior Developer, you will be given room to develop further. You will work together with more experienced colleagues on complex issues and gradually take on more responsibility for technical choices and parts of the environment.
 What you will do 
 As a Software Developer, you will contribute to the further development of our chatAI environment and to applications that are built on top of it.
 You will be involved in, among other things:
 developing and maintaining reliable and user-friendly software;
 building new functionalities within our AI environment;
 developing user applications that help students, teachers and researchers to apply AI in a practical way;
 linking AI models and third-party services to our software;
 developing APIs, integrations, and backend and frontend components;
 writing automated tests and ensuring proper documentation;
 thinking along about software architecture, security, privacy and scalability;
 research and application of new technologies and development methods;
 experimenting with AI-assisted software development and critically assessing its results;
 collaborate with colleagues from IT, education, research and other disciplines and institutions/universities to translate technical solutions into concrete user needs.
 You don't just work on the technology itself. You will also help determine how we make AI available to our university community in a responsible way.
 The reason why you want to work with us 
 You will work in a professional IT organization with more than 200 IT specialists where you can learn a lot and develop yourself well. We have two of our own data centers on our beautiful, green and spacious campus and we are committed to digital sovereignty, the use of open source solutions and a sustainable use of our environment. You will work with state-of-the-art technology in the field of Artificial Intelligence and the infrastructure used for this.
 You will be working on technology that will play a major role in education and research in the coming years.
 You build software that is actually used by students, teachers and researchers.
 You work with modern AI technology and are given the space to experiment with it.
 You will work on interesting issues in the field of software engineering, AI, security, privacy and user experience.
 You will have the opportunity to further develop yourself both technically and substantively.
 You will work in an environment where knowledge sharing, learning and collaboration are central.
 Your qualities
 What we expect from you
 AI is not only changing the applications we build, but also the way software is developed. AI-assisted development can help with code writing, testing, and documentation, as well as solution exploration. Therefore, we don't expect you to write all code yourself from an empty file. However, we do expect you to understand what you are building, to be able to critically assess what AI is generating and to take responsibility for the quality of the software you deliver.
 You don't have to be an AI expert. Experience with LLMs, prompt engineering, AI agents, RAG or other AI technologies is a plus, but not a requirement. We think it's more important that you are a good software developer who is curious about what makes AI possible Curiosity, a good software engineering foundation and the willingness to learn new technology are more important to us.
 What do you take with you? 
 HBO working and thinking level, preferably through a completed HBO ICT education.
 several years of professional experience in software development, or comparable demonstrable experience;
 a good foundation in software engineering, including testing, version control, code quality, and software design;
 experience with a modern technology stack such as TypeScript, React and Next.js, Docker, GitHub Actions, and Azure
 experience with APIs, databases, and software integrations;
 the ability to independently develop functionality within an existing architecture;
 interest in Artificial Intelligence and the developments surrounding generative AI;
 the ability to quickly master and critically assess new technology;
 attention to security, privacy, reliability and maintainability;
 Good communication skills and pleasure in working with people from different backgrounds.
 A good command of Dutch and English. 
 We are looking for a developer who can work independently, but also actively seeks cooperation when a question is complex.
 We offer
 Wageningen University & Research offers excellent terms of employment . A few highlights from our Collective Labour Agreement include:
 working hours that can be discussed and arranged so that they allow for the best possible work-life balance;
 sabbatical leave, study leave, and partially paid parental leave;
 there is a strong focus on vitality and you can make use of the sports facilities available on campus for a small fee;
 a fixed year-end bonus of 8,3%;
 excellent pension scheme.
 We encourage development and internal mobility within our organisation. Our recruitment and selection policy sets out the conditions that apply specifically to you as a (former) employee. If you have any questions, we are happy to help.
 Of course, these excellent fringe benefits also include an appropriate salary. Depending on your experience, we offer a salary of at least € 3,554 to a maximum of € 5,698 for a full working week of 36 hours according to the Collective Labor Agreement Wageningen Research (scale 10). An appointment of 32 hours is negotiable for this role. When it comes to your contract, we initially offer a one-year contract with the intention of converting it to an indefinite contract. 
 We encourage development and mobility within our organization. In our  recruitment and selection policy,   you can read which conditions apply specifically to you as a (former) employee. If you have any questions about this, we are happy to help you.
 You will work on the greenest and most innovative campus in the Netherlands, in an international and open working atmosphere. 
 Important information
 For more information about the position, please contact Diana Hoogeveen, Department Manager Development, on 06-81182860 or by e-mail: diana.hoogeveen@wur.nl 
Questions about the procedure? Please contact Edvard Jongschaap, Senior Corporate Recruiter, 0317 – 489 178 or via email: edvard.jongschaap@wur.nl 
 Apply?  
Click on the application button for the vacancy on our website. Onl

wur:16716.description:
PhD position in modelling the formation and flooding of coastal peat landscapes
Your job
 Are you fascinated by how water, sediment and vegetation shape coastal landscapes? Would you like to use numerical models to reconstruct the formation and flooding of peat landscapes during the Holocene, to better understand coastal landscape development under future sea-level rise? And are you eager to work in an interdisciplinary research team? Then this PhD position may be for you! 
 Zeeland is a low-lying coastal province in the south-western part of the Netherlands. During the Holocene, large parts of the region developed into an extensive peat landscape. Following a combination of sea-level rise, natural landscape dynamics and human land use, this landscape was flooded and transformed into the tidal environment it is today.
 As a PhD candidate, you will investigate the processes behind these major landscape changes. You will translate existing geological records and paleo-tidal reconstructions into morphological and hydrodynamic boundary conditions for new numerical model simulations. In turn, you will validate the simulated coastal landscape development with the available paleo-records.
 You will work with numerical models such as Delft3D, to capture interactions between hydrodynamics, sediment transport, morphology, and peat dynamics. The model studies will help to unravel how these bio-physical interactions have, over centuries to millennia, formed the Zeeland peat landscape and caused its subsequent rapid flooding. 
 This PhD position is part of  Re-Flex Zeeland , an interdisciplinary project funded by the  Delta Climate Center . The project brings together Wageningen University, Utrecht University, the Royal Netherlands Institute for Sea Research (NIOZ), and regional partners. Although the project focuses on Zeeland, it addresses internationally relevant questions about the resilience and vulnerability of low-lying deltas under sea-level rise, land subsidence and human intervention.
 Within the Re-Flex Zeeland project, you will collaborate closely with another PhD candidate working on geological and vegetation reconstructions, and with a postdoctoral researcher developing three-dimensional visualizations of the past landscapes.
 Your duties and responsibilities include:
 developing, calibrating and evaluating numerical models that couple hydrodynamics, sediment transport, morphology and vegetation dynamics;
 translating geological and paleo-tidal reconstructions into model configurations;
 designing numerical experiments to investigate the formation and flooding of coastal peat landscapes;
 collaborating with researchers at Wageningen University, Utrecht University, NIOZ and the Delta Climate Center;
 publishing your findings in scientific journals and presenting them at international conferences;
 supervising BSc and MSc thesis students, and contributing to field excursions in Zeeland as part of BSc and MSc education;
 completing a PhD thesis.
 You will work here
 We are the  Hydrology and Environmental Hydraulics Group (HWM), a dynamic and committed team of academics, support staff, postdocs and PhD researchers. Our focus is on hydrology and environmental hydraulics, which we combine to understand and quantify hydrological processes in catchments, aquifers, rivers and deltas. We develop novel sensing techniques, create models and perform field and lab experiments to advance process understanding.
 You will be supervised by  Roeland van de Vijsel and  Ton Hoitink , and will work closely together with other researchers in the Re-Flex Zeeland project.
 Your working time will be divided between Wageningen University, and the Delta Climate Center in Vlissingen, a coastal city in Zeeland. The exact balance will be agreed at the start of the appointment. Regular presence at both locations and travel between them will be expected.
 Your qualities
 For this project, we are looking for a highly motivated PhD candidate. You are curious and analytical and have a strong interest in coastal and delta landscapes. You are enthusiastic about linking geological data with numerical modelling and can work independently while contributing to an interdisciplinary team.
 You also have:
 a completed MSc degree in earth sciences, hydraulic or coastal engineering, physical oceanography, physical geography, or a related field;
 a strong background in hydrodynamics, morphodynamics or sediment transport;
 experience with numerical modelling;
 programming skills in, for example, Python, MATLAB, or similar;
 experience with spatial or time-series data;
 good scientific writing and presentation skills;
 willingness to work regularly in both Wageningen and Vlissingen.
 For this position your command of the English language is expected to be at C1  level . Sometimes it is necessary to submit an internationally recognised Certificate of Proficiency in the English Language. More information can be found  here . 
 Knowledge of the Dutch language if preferred. 
 We offer
 Wageningen University & Research offers excellent  terms of employment . A few highlights from our Collective Labour Agreement include:
 Partially paid parental leave;
 working hours that can be discussed and arranged so that they allow for the best possible work-life balance;
 there is a strong focus on vitality and you can make use of the sports facilities available on campus for a small fee;
 a fixed year-end bonus of 8.3%;
 excellent pension scheme.
 In addition to these first-rate employee benefits, you will receive a fully funded PhD position and you will be offered a course program tailored to your needs and the research team.
The gross salary for the first year is € 3.204,- per month rising to € 4.051,-  in the fourth year in according to the  Collective Labour Agreements for Dutch Universities (CAO-NU)  (scale P). This is based on a full-time working week of 38 hours. We offer a temporary contract for 18 months which will be extended for the duration of the project if you perform well. 
 We encourage development and internal mobility within our organisation. Our recruitment and selection policy sets out the conditions that apply specifically to you as a (former) employee. If you have any questions, we are happy to help.
 You will work on the greenest and most innovative campus in the Netherlands, in an international and open working environment. 
 Coming from abroad
 Wageningen University & Research is the university and research centre for life sciences. The themes we deal with are relevant to everyone around the world and Wageningen, therefore, has a large international community and a lot to offer to international employees. 
 Because we expect you to work and live in the Netherlands our team of advisors on Dutch immigration procedures will help you with the visa application procedures for yourself and, if applicable, for your family. 
 Feeling welcome also has everything to do with being well informed. Wageningen University & Research's  International Community  page contains practical information about what we can do to support international employees coming to Wageningen. Furthermore, our  Welcome Center can assist you with any additional advice and information about for example housing, opening a bank account, dual career programs or schooling. Finally, certain categories of international staff may be eligible for a tax exemption on a part of their salary during the first five years in the Netherlands.
 Important information
 For more information about the position, please contact  Roeland van de Vijsel , Assistant Professor of Environmental Hydraulics, or  Ton Hoitink , Professor of Earth System Sciences.
 Questions about the procedure? Get in touch with Jessa Rozema, Corporate Recruiter, via jessa.rozema-stol@wur.nl 
 Ready to apply? 
You can apply directly using the apply button on the vacancy page on our website which will allow us to process your personal information with your approval. Only appl

wur:16742.description:
We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher to work on the governance of transitions towards more sustainable and regenerative farming systems. Governance instruments — including certification schemes, private standards, and policy incentives — play an important role in shaping the future of livestock farming systems. Are you curious about how standards, incentives, and sustainability indicators influence farming systems and farmers’ livelihoods? Are you excited to contribute to interdisciplinary research that supports transitions towards more sustainable farming systems? Then we would love to hear from you. 
We are looking for a candidate with experience in the governance of farming systems, who is highly motivated to contribute to more sustainable food systems through inter- and transdisciplinary research. You are a systems thinker with an interest in public-private governance of livestock farming systems, agricultural policy and/or sustainable finance. You are familiar with current policy debates on the future of European and Dutch agriculture and enjoy working at the interface of science, policy and practice.
You will strengthen the team working on farming systems governance by contributing to research on public and private governance instruments, sustainability indicators, and perceptions of those affected by transitions. Your work will include contributing to ongoing research on regenerative agriculture (including ReGeNL), facilitating stakeholder engagement, supporting project management, developing research proposals, and contributing to education. You are encouraged to describe relevant experience with these tasks in your CV and motivation letter.
 You will work here 
This postdoc will be situated at the Animal Production Systems group of Wageningen University & Research. Here we analyze and explore the roles of livestock in food systems with the aim of making these food systems more sustainable. The group focuses on unravelling the complexity of livestock systems, and exploring trade-offs and synergies of innovations regarding environmental impact, animal welfare, and economic viability, to design a more sustainable future.
You will join the team led by Dr. Evelien de Olde and contribute to research on governance issues in farming systems within a polycentric food systems context, including sustainability certification and standards, the role of metrics and KPIs, and the impact of sustainability transitions on livestock farming systems.

REQUIREMENTS:
A completed PhD degree with an interdisciplinary focus in a relevant field such as sustainability science, agricultural science, animal science, political science, environmental science, or related disciplines;
 Curiosity to explore leverage points and complexities in the governance of farming systems while working in transdisciplinary settings with project partners including farmers;
 Affinity with farming systems research, sustainability indicators, and governance theories ;
 Experience with diverse qualitative and/or mixed-method research methods such as interviews, focus groups, serious games, and stakeholder engagement;
 Interest in developing research proposals and contributing to future projects;
 You are a good team player, collaborative and creative who can communicate easily with diverse audiences and act proactively;
 You posses strong communication skills, both verbal and written, in Dutch and English (C1 level);
 You are able and willing to work from Wageningen at least 3 days a week.

CONTRACT: 2 years

wur:16744.description:
Do you enjoy variety in your work, want to contribute to fish and fisheries research, and enjoy collaborating with colleagues? Then we have an exciting opportunity for you at Wageningen Marine Research as a laboratory technician in fish biology! A good command of the Dutch language is essential for this role.
As a laboratory technician in fish biology, you will support fish and fisheries research through your practical experience, species identification skills and accurate working methods. You will help ensure biological samples are processed reliably and that researchers have access to high-quality data.
 Your duties and responsibilities as a Laboratory technician fish biology include: 
 processing biological samples in WMR’s research laboratories;
 determining the age of various fish species, including preparing samples and contributing to the development of fish ageing methods;
 recording research data, identifying and reporting any anomalies, and providing data to researchers;
 archiving and managing biological sample collections and research materials;
 coordinating sampling logistics, both in the laboratory and at field locations throughout the Netherlands.
 Your team 
You will work with colleagues across various fish research project teams. Your work will support a broad range of studies, including larval fish research and diet studies. You will combine taking initiative to improve working methods with carrying out routine laboratory tasks.
 You will work here 
You will be based at Wageningen Marine Research in IJmuiden, working in its offices and research laboratories.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an accurate, proactive and solution-oriented team player with good communication skills. You switch easily between tasks and work effectively both independently and as part of a team. You have a natural aptitude for organising tasks, materials, and samples in a systematic, structured manner.
 You also possess: 
 a Dutch senior secondary vocational qualification at level 4 (MBO-4), or an equivalent qualification, in a biological discipline, and an interest in fish and fisheries research; Applicants with a university degree are excluded from the selection process.
 demonstrable experience in biological laboratory work, preferably in a research environment; experience working with fish and other marine species is an advantage;
 proficiency in Dutch at a minimum of CEFR level B2;
 the attention to detail required to interpret and follow sampling and data protocols accurately;
 preferably, a category B driving licence.
In our international working environment there is an increasing amount of communication in English. For this position it is about language level B1.

CONTRACT: 12 months

wur:16746.description:
AI Platform engineer
Your job
 Within WUR, we are setting up our own chatAI environment in which our users (students, teachers, researchers) can use the possibilities of Artificial Intelligence for research and education safely and within established guardrails. This includes the WUR AI Chat, the WUR LLM Hub and integrations with internal data sources. 
 As an AI Platform Engineer, you will be part of an ambitious and technically strong team that is responsible for developing and maintaining our WUR AI Facility. You play a key role in setting up and managing the necessary infrastructure in Azure, provisioning of the CI/CD pipeline, monitoring and improving performance and improving the user experience. The system management covers infrastructure, platform, applications and functional services.
 We are looking for someone with a passion for IT, who has experience in building reliable infrastructure solutions based on Azure cloud solutions and on-premise cloud-native container environments.
 Your tasks and responsibilities as an AI Platform Engineer are: 
 provisioning, managing, and further developing secure, reliable, and scalable Azure and Kubernetes environment(s) for the AI Facility;
 provision and maintenance of CI/CD pipelines and deployment processes, including rollback and release procedures;
 manage environments, infrastructure resources, technical configurations, and dependencies, while ensuring version control and consistent provisioning across all environments;
 documenting technical architecture, management procedures and runbooks;
 automate the use and management. Supporting releases and analyzing technical incidents, malfunctions and performance issues;
 monitoring and improving platform performance, security, manageability and reliability. Setting up and maintaining the necessary monitoring, alerting and observability;
 collaborate with software developers to improve the user experience;
 you build it, you own it: you solve disruptions and performance issues together with your colleagues.
 Your team 
You will work in a team with a product owner, a scrum master, a solution architect, software developers and IT engineers. Our IT teams work according to agile principles. Collaboration, communication, transparency and continuous feedback are essential. You will work on campus at least two days a week and can choose to work partly from home as well, for good focus and a healthy work-life balance.
 You will work here  
You will work in a professional IT organization with more than 200 IT specialists where you can learn a lot and develop yourself well. We have two of our own data centers on our beautiful, green and spacious campus and we are committed to digital sovereignty, the use of open source solutions and a sustainable use of our environment. You will work with state-of-the-art technology in the field of Artificial Intelligence and the infrastructure used for this. 
 Your qualities
 You are an independent and inquisitive IT professional with a DevOps mindset. You like to work together with other professionals and different stakeholders in an agile environment. You are a connector and dare to speak out and ask questions. You are a real go-getter, have strong communication skills and have a warm heart for education and research. In addition, you are analytically minded but also solution- and customer-oriented. You are enterprising and have the drive and organizational talent to get things done as a driving force. You are creative and always look for new solutions to make existing environments more effective and new functionalities possible. You also have a good eye for the availability, stability, scalability and user-friendliness of the infrastructure provided. 
 You also possess:  
 HBO working and thinking level, preferably through a completed HBO ICT education;
 experience as a System Administrator or DevOps Engineer in an enterprise environment;
 knowledge of and experience with Linux and Kubernetes environments and Azure cloud solutions;
 knowledge of designing and managing cloud-native concepts;
 experience with infrastructure-as-code (e.g. Terraform, Ansible) and a declarative mindset;
 experience with containerization and CI/CD tools (e.g. ArgoCD, Git(lab));
 basic knowledge of programming and scripting languages such as Python or Bash;
 a good command of Dutch and English. The working language within the IT organization is Dutch, but in our international working environment, more and more communication is in English.
 You can see that there are plenty of challenges for you. These challenges can also be seen as development opportunities. So if you do not yet have the track record required in the vacancy, but do have solid work experience, we would also like to invite you to apply.
 We offer
 Wageningen University & Research offers excellent  terms of employment . A few highlights from our Collective Labour Agreement include:
 working hours that can be discussed and arranged so that they allow for the best possible work-life balance;
 sabbatical leave, study leave, and partially paid parental leave;
 there is a strong focus on vitality and you can make use of the sports facilities available on campus for a small fee;
 a fixed year-end bonus of 8,3%;
 excellent pension scheme.
 Of course, these excellent fringe benefits also include an appropriate salary. Depending on your experience, we offer a salary of a minimum of € 3,554.- to a maximum of € 6,471.- for a full working week of 36 hours according to Collective Labor Agreement Wageningen Research (scale 10/11). For this role, an appointment of 32 hours is also negotiable. We initially offer a one-year contract, with the intention of converting it to an indefinite contract
 We encourage development and internal mobility within our organisation. Our recruitment and selection policy sets out the conditions that apply specifically to you as a (former) employee. If you have any questions, we are happy to help.
 You will work on the greenest and most innovative campus in the Netherlands, in an international and open working atmosphere. 
