wur:16772.description:
Are you fascinated by how eukaryotic genomes evolve? Do you want to look beyond model organisms and point mutations to uncover the complexity of genome evolution? Are you excited to use computational approaches to explore how genome architecture shapes evolution in a collaborative and stimulating environment? We offer a PhD position in our diverse research team in the Laboratory of Microbiology at Wageningen University & Research. As a PhD candidate, you will investigate how 3D chromosome folding influence how the genomes of unicellular eukaryotes evolve. 
 What will you do? 
In this PhD project, you will study the genome evolution and architecture – including 3D folding - of understudied unicellular eukaryotes, using the latest computational approaches.
 As PhD candidate in our lab, you will: 
 utilize cutting-edge bioinformatics tools to trace the evolution of genomes, genes and proteins, including phylogenetic analyses and reconstructions;
 use new genomic data to characterize the large-scale and small-scale genome architectures of understudied eukaryotic microbes;
 deepen our understanding of eukaryotes’ diversification and origins;
 have the opportunity to pursue your own research questions and implement your ideas;
 work independently and develop your own research questions within the project;
 contribute to teaching in our courses and supervising BSc and MSc thesis students;
 work in a stimulating environment that combines evolutionary and cellular research on eukaryotes and prokaryotes, in a team operating at the forefront of unravelling the deep origins of eukaryotic cells, and we are expanding our explorations of the understudied world of eukaryotic microbes.
Even though this is a computational project, it offers possibilities to work on eukaryotic microbes in the wet lab.
 Why? 
Understanding how genomes evolve is fundamental to explaining species diversity and adaptation. Compared with prokaryotes, eukaryotic genomes show a greater structural and functional plasticity, with a larger genome size, a higher proportion of non-coding regions and a more complex 3D chromosome organization. Notably, genome evolution is not only driven by small mutations, but also by larger changes, including chromosome rearrangements and horizontal gene transfer. How and why such changes occur is still an open question, in particular for eukaryotic microbes, which dominate the eukaryotic tree of life but remain poorly studied. Recent studies, conducted in model organisms, suggest that these changes are induced by how chromosomes are folded in 3D. This 3D folding of the chromosomes typically allows distant genomic regions to interact. To gain a deeper understanding of genome evolution, including in non-model organisms, taking 3D chromosome folding into account is a promising direction.
 You will work here 
This PhD position is embedded within the Laboratory of Microbiology , and part of the Genome Evolution and Dynamics in Eukaryotes (GEDE) group, which is led by Dr. Jolien van Hooff . You will be co-supervised by Dr. Ursula Oggenfuss. We aim to get this position filled by March 2027.

REQUIREMENTS:
You are an enthusiastic and curiosity-driven candidate with a strong interest in evolution, genomics and microbiology, especially for non-model protists. You have at least some experience with handling biological datasets and a strong drive to learn and apply bioinformatic approaches. Preferably, you have some knowledge of genome-wide analyses and phylogenetics. If you bring experience or a strong interest in the cultivation of protists, we are open to the possibility of integrating cultivation as part of your project.
 You also possess: 
 a MSc degree in Biology, Microbiology, Bioinformatics, or a related field by the start of the project;
 programming experience with languages like Python and R and/or experience in bioinformatics analyses;
 a strong curiosity in learning about biological systems and to work interdisciplinary;
 good organization skills and a high level of independence;
 good verbal and written communication skills, as well as the willingness and ability to work together in teams.
For this position your command of the English language is expected to be at C1 level . Sometimes it is necessary to submit an internationally recognized Certificate of Proficiency in the English Language. More information can be found here .

CONTRACT: 18 months

wur:16833.description:
Have you ever asked yourself if it is possible to harness the economic potential of biodiversity without degrading the environment? Would you like to understand how we can develop markets for native plant species that benefit rural communities while conserving biodiversity? Are you proficient in Portuguese? Then this PhD might be your next step! 
The Cerrado is one of the world's biodiversity hotspots but it is increasingly threatened by agricultural expansion and global market pressures, creating tensions between conservation, economic development, and local livelihoods. Native Cerrado species such as macaúba, buriti and pequi not only have an important ecological role for the local ecosystems, but also have the potential to provide food, income, and other livelihood opportunities to the local communities
This PhD position is part of the research project “ Leveraging biodiversity for reshaping agricultural production in the Brazilian Cerrado ”, which examines how native species can be integrated into farming systems to support biodiversity and improve local livelihoods without further conversion of natural vegetation. The project brings together the expertise from researchers in Brazil and the Netherlands to map current production of native species and their value chains, assess promising business opportunities, estimate future income potential, and test traceability approaches together with farmers, cooperatives, and companies.
Your research will focus on understanding how native species are currently produced, managed and commercialized by smallholder farmers in the MATOPIBA region. By integrating data from primary household/farm surveys and secondary geospatial datasets, and using advanced econometric methods, you will evaluate if greater production diversity involving native species, the adoption of sustainable production practices, and specific market configurations can contribute to food security and biodiversity outcomes. You will have the opportunity to develop your own research questions within the broader objectives of the project. Start date: February 2027.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 design and implement large-scale household and farm-level surveys with smallholder farmers in the MATOPIBA region to collect socioeconomic and biodiversity field data;
 analyze geospatial and survey data using rigorous econometric methods ;
 conduct workshops and focus group discussions with local stakeholders to analyze the political economy of current markets for native species;
 write research papers and present research findings at international academic conferences;
 collaborate with our research partners and contribute to the dissemination of project results .
 You will work here 
You will be based at the Forest and Nature Conservation Policy Group (FNP) at Wageningen University. The FNP group focuses on the political, socio-economic and cultural dimensions of forests and nature. Our research addresses major challenges including biodiversity conservation, deforestation, forest transitions, climate change and landscape management in both the Global North and Global South. You will be supervised by Dr. Jorge Sellare and Dr Verina Ingram .

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a researcher with a strong interest in agricultural economics, rural development, biodiversity conservation and sustainable land use. You enjoy working with quantitative data and are motivated to conduct research that combines rigorous econometric analysis with real-world challenges facing farmers and rural communities. Fieldwork in Brazil will be an important component of the PhD project, so you should be enthusiastic about working with farmers and able to spend time in rural areas under fieldwork conditions.
 You also possess: 
 a successfully completed MSc degree in agricultural economics, development economics, agricultural sciences or a closely related field;
 affinity with causal inference and an interest in developing your skills in impact evaluation and applied econometrics;
 good skills with statistical software such as R, Stata or Python;
 experience with data collection in rural areas, including field biodiversity data;
 proven excellent communication skills in Portuguese (level C1);
 knowledge of GIS and spatial data analysis is an advantage.
For this position your command of the English language is expected to be at C1 level . Sometimes it is necessary to submit an internationally recognised Certificate of Proficiency in the English Language. More information can be found here .

CONTRACT: 18 months

wur:16860.description:
Have you ever wondered how we can ensure that climate mitigation and adaptation policies are fair, coherent, and effective? Are you interested in applying state-of-the-art impact evaluation techniques to analyze how climate policies affect land use, GHG emissions, and biodiversity? Do you good communication skills in Spanish? Then this might be your next step! 
Climate change mitigation and adaptation in agriculture and forestry represent a “wicked problem”, shaped by uncertainty, conflicting objectives, institutional fragmentation and time pressure. While the EU has set ambitious climate targets for 2030 and climate neutrality by 2050, progress in agriculture and forestry remains insufficient and current policy frameworks have not yet resulted in significant climate mitigation and adaptation results, or generated the systemic transformation needed.
This PhD position is part of the EU-Horizon project “ Evidence-based pathways towards fair, coherent and effective climate policies for Europe’s agriculture and forestry (PolicyForClimate) ”, which will develop tools and frameworks to enable policymakers and other stakeholders to design fair, coherent, and effective climate mitigation and adaptation policies in the EU. The project will use AI-assisted evidence synthesis, a policy-lab approach, and embedded case studies to offer a continuously updated evidence base on policy impacts, trade-offs, synergies, and spillovers.
Your research will focus on developing, applying, and validating a novel mixed-methods framework for ex-post impact evaluation of climate policies in forestry and agricultural landscapes. By combining state-of-the-art econometric techniques with qualitative participatory approaches, you will empirically analyze how climate policies have affected land use, GHG emissions, and biodiversity metrics. This will require conducting systematic reviews on existing impact evaluation frameworks, run workshops and focus group discussions with various stakeholders, collect, combine, and analyze farm-level primary data and secondary spatially explicit data.
Start date: February 2027.
 Your duties and responsibilities include: 
 Conduct systematic literature reviews and workshops to develop a mixed-methods ex-post impact evaluation framework for climate policies;
 Apply rigorous econometric methods together with qualitative assessments , particularly from policy sciences, to evaluate the impact of specific climate policies on land cover, carbon, and biodiversity metrics;
 Conduct focus group discussions with various stakeholders in the EU to validate the proposed impact evaluation framework and results from the empirical analyses;
 Write research papers and present research findings at international academic conferences;
 Collaborate with our research partners and contribute to the dissemination of project results .
 You will work here 
You will be based at the Forest and Nature Conservation Policy Group (FNP) at Wageningen University . The FNP group focuses on the political, socio-economic and cultural dimensions of forests and nature. Our research addresses major challenges including biodiversity conservation, deforestation, forest transitions, climate change and landscape management in both the Global North and Global South. You will be jointly supervised by Dr. Jorge Sellare and Prof. Bas Arts from Wageningen University (WU-FNP), and by Dr. Dario Schulz (EFI) in the research group headed by Sven Wunder.

REQUIREMENTS:
We are looking for a researcher with a strong interest in climate adaptation and mitigation policies and in exploring the underlying causal mechanisms through which they affect land, GHG emissions, and biodiversity. You enjoy working with both quantitative and qualitative data and are motivated to conduct research that combines rigorous econometric analysis with qualitative assessments and with real-world challenges facing farmers and forest owners. Fieldwork in Spain will be an important component of the PhD project, so Spanish language skills are critical. You should be enthusiastic about working with farmers and able to spend time in rural areas of Spain under fieldwork conditions.
 You also possess: 
 a successfully completed MSc degree in agricultural economics, environmental economics, environmental policy, forestry or a closely related field;
 affinity with applied econometrics and / or qualitative mixed methods and an interest in developing your skills in both;
 knowledge of GIS and spatial data analysis is an asset;
 good skills with statistical software such as R, Stata or Python;
 very good communication skills in Spanish (min. level B2);
For this position your command of the English language is expected to be at C1 level . Sometimes it is necessary to submit an internationally recognised Certificate of Proficiency in the English Language. More information can be found here .

